\begin{abstract}

Diffusion models achieve realistic outcomes across a wide range of generative tasks, but their high computational cost remains a major barrier to deployment. Model pruning has emerged as a promising strategy to reduce inference cost and enable lightweight diffusion models. While effective, pruned diffusion models are proned to quality reduction due to limited capacity. A key limitation of current pruning approaches is that pruned models are finetuned using the same objective as the dense model, typically denoising score matching (DSM). Since the dense model is accessible during finetuning, it warrants a more effective approach for knowledge transfer from the dense to the pruned model. Motivated by this aim, we revisit the finetuning stage and propose IGSM (\textbf{I}mproved \textbf{G}eometric and \textbf{S}ensitivity \textbf{M}atching), a general-purpose finetuning framework that introduces a second-order Jacobian projection loss inspired by Finite-Time Lyapunov Exponents (FTLE). IGSM efficiently captures and aligns the geometric and the temporal dynamics of pruned models with their dense teachers using scalable second-order projections. Our approach is architecture-agnostic and applies to both U-Net- and Transformer-based diffusion models. Experiments on CIFAR-10, CelebA, LSUN-Church, and LSUN-Bedroom show that IGSM consistently narrows the performance gap between pruned and dense models, substantially improving sample quality. Code is available on GitHub: \hyperlink{https://github.com/FATE4869/IGSM-Official}{https://github.com/FATE4869/IGSM-Official} 

\end{abstract}
\vspace{-2mm}
\section{Introduction}
\vspace{-2mm}
\label{introduction}
Diffusion models have demonstrated remarkable success across a wide range of generative tasks~\citep{ho2020denoising}, including text-to-image~\citep{saharia2022photorealistic}, image-to-image translation~\citep{parmar2023zero}, video generation~\citep{yang2023diffusion}, and super-resolution~\citep{li2022srdiff}. However, this success comes at a significant computational cost: the typical sampling process involves hundreds of iterative denoising steps, each requires a forward pass through a large backbone architecture such as U-Net~\citep{rombach2022high} or Transformer~\citep{peebles2023scalable, bao2023all}. Such computational burden presents a major challenge for real-time deployment and applications in resource-constrained environments.


To mitigate the computational cost, recent efforts have approached the improvement of the efficiency of diffusion models mainly from two complementary directions: i) reducing the number of denoising steps~\citep{song2020denoising, nichol2021improved}, and ii) compressing backbone architectures to decrease computational cost per step~\citep{shang2023post, li2023q, kim2023architectural}. Within the compression paradigm, pruning has emerged as a promising strategy for enabling lightweight, hardware-efficient diffusion models as it reduces model size and Multiply-Accumulate Operations (MACs) directly. A growing body of work has proposed pruning techniques specifically tailored for diffusion models, incorporating structured sparsity, block-wise importance scoring, trajectory-aware objectives, and subnetwork searching~\citep{fang2023structural, castells2024ld, zhang2024effortless, zhu2024dip}. 


Despite these advantages, pruned diffusion models still exhibit a noticeable quality degradation compared to their dense counterparts~\citep{zhu2024dip}. A key reason for this gap is that most pruning methods retain the same training objective used for dense models — typically denoising score matching (DSM), which supervises the model to predict the added noise at each timestep. While effective for training full-capacity models, this objective may not be a sufficient guide for pruned models, which operate with reduced capacity and altered internal dynamics.


In this work, we propose to revisit the finetuning objective in the context of pruning. Specifically, since the dense model remains accessible during finetuning, it offers a unique opportunity to supervise the pruned model in a more effective way. Prior works have explored output- and feature-level knowledge distillation, aligning predictions or intermediate activations between the pruned and the dense models~\citep{kim2023architectural}. While such strategies offer quality improvements, they still treat the dense model primarily as a source of soft labels and fail to fully leverage its rich internal representations, particularly how the score function evolves temporally and responds to local perturbations. 

A possible natural alternative objective is Jacobian matching, which aligns the gradients of the score function (i.e., the derivative of the predicted noise with respect to the input) between the pruned and the dense model. Figure.~\ref{fig:demo} illustrates the difference between score matching and Jacobian matching. In Figure.~\ref{fig:demo}, the middle column visualizes the score field for three cases: 1) top: trained with score matching, 2) middle: the ground-truth score field, and 3) bottom: trained with Jacobian matching. While score matching encourages the model to match the magnitude of the score vectors, it does not necessarily constrain how these vectors deform the distribution, i.e., it overlooks the geometric expansion or contraction. Over time, this can potentially lead to sampling drift. In contrast, Jacobian matching explicitly imposes supervision in local geometric deformation, resulting in a final distribution that more closely resembles the ground truth.

