While direct (first-order) Jacobian-based supervision appears promising and has shown success in settings like classification and reinforcement learning~\citep{czarnecki2017sobolev}, it is considerably less effective in diffusion models. Under DSM framework, supervision is already applied to noise-corrupted inputs, making output-level matching between dense and pruned models mathematically equivalent to first-order Jacobian matching~\citep{srinivas2018knowledge}. This equivalence diminishes the value of explicitly matching Jacobians, while incurring additional computational overhead. Beyond efficiency concern, diffusion models operate as discrete-time dynamical systems, where sample quality depends not only on local/one-step sensitivity but on how perturbations propagate over time. First-order Jacobians capture only instantaneous responses at individual timesteps and fail to reflect the long-range temporal behavior that governs the stability and fidelity of the denoising trajectory.


To address these issues, we propose \textbf{IGSM} (\textbf{\textit{I}}mproved \textbf{\textit{G}}eometric and \textbf{\textit{S}}ensitivity \textbf{\textit{M}}atching), a general-purpose finetuning objective designed for pruned diffusion models specifically. IGSM leverages the Jacobian in a principled and effective manner: rather than aligning first-order derivatives directly, it matches the second-order geometric behavior of the pruned model with that of the dense teacher. Specifically, IGSM supervises how the score function responds to small input perturbations over time, capturing geometric and temporal sensitivity. This formulation draws inspiration from the theory of Finite-Time Lyapunov Exponents (FTLE), which characterize the local expansion or contraction rates of infinitesimal perturbations evolving over finite horizons. FTLE provides a natural theoretical framework for quantifying how small deviations in input space propagate through the denoising trajectory, thus offering valuable insights into sampling stability and sensitivity. 
% Moreover, since IGSM uses Jacobian matching, it is architecture-agnostic and applicable to a broad range of diffusion model structures.

The computation of FTLE for high-dimensional diffusion models is computationally prohibitive, as it requires evaluating and tracking full Jacobians across multiple timesteps. To maintain scalability, IGSM employs second-order Jacobian projections along randomly sampled directions, estimating perturbation propagation across the denoising process.

Our contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We identify key limitations in existing finetuning approaches for pruned diffusion models, including those that rely on output-level knowledge distillation or first-order Jacobian matching, which do not leverage fully the dense model’s structural and dynamical information.
    \item We propose \textbf{IGSM} (\textbf{\textit{I}}mproved \textbf{\textit{G}}eometric and \textbf{\textit{S}}ensitivity \textbf{\textit{M}}atching), a unified, architecture-agnostic finetuning objective that introduces a second-order Jacobian projection loss inspired by Finite-Time Lyapunov Exponents (FTLE). IGSM efficiently captures and aligns the geometric and temporal dynamics of pruned models with their dense counterparts through scalable random projections.
    \item Our experiments demonstrate that IGSM consistently narrows the quality gap between pruned and dense models, and outperforms existing pruning approaches across both U-Net-based and Transformer-based diffusion architectures, evaluated on datasets such as CIFAR-10, CelebA, LSUN-Church, and LSUN-Bedroom.
\end{itemize}

\vspace{-2mm}
\section{Related Work}
\vspace{-2mm}
\textbf{Efficient Diffusion Models.}
Diffusion models have achieved impressive generative performance but are also known for being expensive to compute due to their iterative denoising process, which involves multiples of forward passes through large backbone models such as U-Nets~\citep{rombach2022high} or Transformers~\citep{peebles2023scalable}. One line of work propose to reduce the number of denoising steps to accelerate the inference, using strategies such as schedule optimization~\citep{watson2021learning}, ODE/SDE-based solver~\citep{song2020denoising, song2020score, lu2022dpm, zhang2022fast, karras2022elucidating}, learning alternative objective~\citep{dockhorn2022genie, watson2022learning} and distillation-based sampler~\citep{salimans2022progressive, luhman2021knowledge}. Another set of approaches reduce inference cost by alternating between different models~\citep{liu2023oms, pan2024t, zhang2024accelerating} or caching intermediate features~\citep{agarwal2024approximate, ma2024deepcache, wimbauer2024cache}. Despite their effectiveness, these approaches typically require additional storage or increased memory during inference, limiting their practical use in resource-constrained settings. To address both computational and storage efficiency without additional memory demands, recent efforts focus on compressing the backbone model itself, typically through quantization, knowledge distillation, or pruning~\citep{shang2023post, li2023q, kim2023architectural, fang2023structural}. 

Among compression strategies, pruning has recently gained significant attention, with several methods tailored specifically for diffusion models. Diff-Pruning applied structured pruning, removing entire groups of weights to enable hardware-friendly acceleration~\citep{fang2023structural}. LD-Pruner proposed to evaluate the importance of fundamental building blocks (e.g., convolution and attention layers) based on their influence on the latent vector statistics relative to the dense model~\citep{castells2024ld}. LAPTOP-Diff adopted a one-shot layer pruning criterion based on output loss differences, followed by finetuning with both output-level and normalized feature-level distillation~\citep{zhang2024laptop}. EcoDiff introduced a differentiable pruning mask trained with an end-to-end objective across the entire sampling trajectory~\citep{zhang2024effortless}. While it accounts for cumulative effects over time, it does not explicitly regularize the temporal dynamics of the score function, i.e., how sensitivity to perturbations evolves across denoising steps. Alternatively, DiP-GO casted pruning as a subnetwork search problem, employing a lightweight pruner network to predict importance scores for each block and removing non-contributing blocks~\citep{zhu2024dip}. While these approaches improve efficiency, a notable quality gap between pruned and dense models remains.

One promising direction for narrowing this gap lies in improving the finetuning objective. Most existing methods finetune pruned models using Denoising Score Matching (DSM), which trains the model to predict the added noise at each timestep. Some methods incorporate knowledge distillation by aligning the outputs or intermediate activations of pruned model with those of the dense model~\citep{kim2023architectural}. While these techniques reduce the gap, they do not fully exploit the dense model's capacity. We argue that more effective use of the dense model, beyond soft labels, is possible, particularly in guiding the temporal behavior and geometric structure of the pruned model's score function.

Notably, our work does not introduce a new pruning algorithm. Instead, it introduces a general-purpose finetuning objective that can be applied on top of any existing pruning method, providing richer supervision for capturing both local sensitivity and time-evolving dynamics, thereby improving stability and sample quality beyond what noise prediction alone can achieve.

\textbf{Score Matching and Jacobian Matching.}
Score-based generative models approximate the gradient of the log-density, i.e., score, of the data distribution~\citep{hyvarinen2005estimation}. In diffusion models, this is commonly achieved via DSM, which provides strong global supervision across timesteps. However, DSM does not supervise how the score function evolves over time, leaving the temporal dynamics of the model under-constrained, an issue that becomes more pronounced in pruned models with reduced capacity.


To strengthen the supervision signal, prior work has explored derivative-based alignment. Sobolev Training showed both theoretically and empirically that incorporating higher-order derivatives of the teacher model can improve learning performance in supervised settings~\citep{czarnecki2017sobolev}. Even first-order Jacobian matching, which is mathematically equivalent to matching outputs on noisy inputs, has been shown to be effective in image classification~\citep{srinivas2018knowledge} and sequential architectures such recurrent neural networks~\citep{smith2021reverse}. However, this technique does not translate well to diffusion models, which are both stochastic and sequential in nature. The stochasticity of the process makes first-order Jacobian matching effectively equivalent to output-level knowledge distillation, while also introducing additional computational cost. Moreover, diffusion models operate over many timesteps, and their generative performance depends not only on instantaneous input-output gradients but also on how perturbations evolve across the entire sampling trajectory. In this setting, first-order Jacobian matching fails to capture the longer-term behaviors needed for stable and high-fidelity generation. This motivates a rethinking of finetuning objectives through the lens of dynamical systems theory, where temporal sensitivity and long-range stability can be explicitly regularized.


\textbf{Finite-Time Lyapunov Exponents.}
To better capture perturbation propagation over time, dynamical systems theory provides insightful tools for finetuning objective design. Since diffusion models evolve through successive updates of a score function, they can be naturally modeled as discrete-time dynamical systems, making them amenable to techniques that analyze system sensitivity and stability across time. A classical metric in this domain is the Lyapunov Exponent (LE), which measures the asymptotic rate of divergence or contraction of nearby trajectories in time-independent systems~\citep{ruelle1979ergodic, oseledets2008oseledets, monteforte2010dynamical, engelken2020lyapunov}. LE have been used to study generalization and stability in deep networks~\citep{vogt2024lyapunov, zheng2022hyperpruning} but its assumption of time-independence and requirement of analytical expression of the dynamics limit its applicability to high-dimensional, time-varying models such as diffusion models.


To address these limitations, the Finite-Time Lyapunov Exponent (FTLE) was introduced as a more practical alternative. FTLE, computed via second-order Jacobian, measures the local rate of expansion or contraction over a finite time interval, making it applicable to time-dependent systems. It has been successfully used in fields such as fluid dynamics and biomedical modeling~\citep{shadden2005definition, green2006detection, brunton2010fast, lipinski2010ridge, haller2015lagrangian, krishna2023finite}. However, its use in high-dimensional model remains computational expensive, as it often requires evaluating the full Jacobian over space and time. 


Interestingly, the machine learning community has introduced scalable alternatives for estimating Jacobian-related quantities without explicitly computing full Jacobians. One notable example is Sliced Score Matching (SSM)~\citep{song2020sliced, song2019generative}, which uses random directional projections to estimate gradients of the score function. Inspired by this idea, we develop a \textit{second-order Jacobian projection loss} rooted in FTLE theory. By projecting the Jacobian into a subspace and aligning the geometric behavior between student and teacher models, our method improves the sample quality and stability of pruned models without requiring full Jacobian computation.

\vspace{-2mm}
\section{Background}
\vspace{-2mm}
\label{bakcground}
In this section, we review Finite-time Lyapunov Exponent (FTLE) theory and fundamental concepts in score-based diffusion models.
\vspace{-1mm}
\subsection{Finite-time Lyapunov Exponent}
\vspace{-1mm}
The FTLE characterizes the expansion or contraction rate of infinitesimally close trajectories in time-dependent dynamical systems, governed by $\frac{dx(t)}{dt} = f(x(t), t)$,

where $f(x(t), t): \mathcal{R}^n \times \mathcal{R} \rightarrow \mathcal{R}^n$ describes the system dynamics,  $x(t)\in \mathcal{R}^n$ is the state variable, and $t \in \mathcal{R}$ denotes time. Given an initial condition $x(t_0)$, the trajectory over the interval $[t_0, t_0 + t_1]$, with $t_1 \in \mathcal{R}$, is obtained by numerically integrating the dynamics. We denote the flow map by $\Phi_{t_0}^{t_0 + t_1}: \mathcal{R}^n \rightarrow \mathcal{R}^n$, which maps the initial state $x(t_0)$ to final state.
\begin{align}
    \Phi_{t_0}^{t_0 + t_1}: x(t_0) \rightarrow x(t_0) + \int_{t_0}^{t_0 + t_1}f(x(\tau), \tau) d \tau.
\end{align}
Now, consider a nearby point 
$x^{\prime}(t_0) = x(t_0) + v(t_0)$, where $v(t_0) \in \mathcal{R}^n$ is a infinitesimal perturbation. After a time interval $t_1$, the distance between those two neighboring points becomes
\begin{align}
    v(t_0 + t_1) & = x^{\prime}(t_0 + t_1) - x(t_0 + t_1) 
                      = \frac{d\Phi_{t_0}^{t_0 + t_1}}{dx} v(t_0) + \mathcal{O} (||v(t_0)||^2),
\end{align}
which is expressed in Taylor Expansion form and the second term is negligible for infinitesimal $v(t_0)$. Thus, the magnitude of the distance after time $t_1$ can be approximated by
\begin{align}
    ||v(t_0 + t_1)|| = \sqrt{<v(t_0), J^{\top} J v(t_0)>},
    \label{eq:2nd-order_jac}
\end{align}
where $J = \frac{d\Phi_{t_0}^{t_0 + t_1}}{dx}$ is the Jacobian of the flow map, and $(\cdot)^{\top}$ denotes the matrix transpose operation.

The maximum distance occurs when $v(t_0)$ aligns with the eigenvector associated with the maximum eigenvalue $\lambda_{\max}$ of $(J^{\top} J)$. The resulting maximum perturbation magnitude is $\lambda_{\max} ||v(t_0)||$.

The FTLE is then defined as the average exponential stretching ratio in the direction of maximal stretching, i.e., $\sigma^{t_1}_{t_0}(x) = \frac{1}{t_1}\ln \sqrt{\lambda_{\max}}$.

\vspace{-1mm}
\subsection{Score-based Generative Modeling}
\vspace{-1mm}
Given a dataset $\{x_i\}^D_{i=1}$, we assume there exists an untractable data distribution $p_{\text{data}}(x)$ from which each data point is independently sampled. 
Score-based generative modeling aims to sample from $p_{\text{data}}(x)$ by learning the gradient of the log-probability density $\nabla_x \log p(x)$, also known as the score. A neural network $s(\cdot; \theta):\mathcal{R}^n \rightarrow \mathcal{R}^n$ is trained to approximate this score via the score matching loss.

\begin{align}
    \frac{1}{2} E_{p(x)} \Big[ ||s(x; \theta) - \nabla_x \log p(x)||^2_2 \Big].
    \label{eq:score_matching}
\end{align}
Since the true score $\nabla_x \log p(x)$ is typically intractable, DSM approximates it by training on noise-perturbed data~\citep{vincent2011connection}. Specifically, for a given noise level $\sigma > 0$, noisy samples $\Tilde{x}\sim q_{\sigma}(\Tilde{x}|x)=\mathcal{N}(x, \sigma^2 \mathcal{I})$ are drawn, and the objective becomes

\begin{align}
    l(\theta; \sigma) = \frac{1}{2} E_{\Tilde{x}} [\| s(\Tilde{x}, \sigma;\theta) + \frac{\Tilde{x} - x}{\sigma^2}\|].
\end{align}

Noise Conditional Score Network (NCSN) extends this by estimating a family of score functions across multiple noise levels $\{\sigma_i\}_{i=1}^L$~\citep{song2019generative}. The unified multi-scale training objective is
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{L}(\theta; \{\sigma_i\}_{i=1}^L) = \frac{1}{L} \sum_{i=1}^L \lambda(\sigma_i) l(\theta_i; \sigma),
\end{align}
where $\lambda(\sigma_i)$ is a weighting factor.


In practice, score-based diffusion models often simplify DSM by directly predicting the noise $\epsilon \in \mathcal{N}(0, \mathcal{I})$ added in the forward diffusion process using a Mean Square Error loss: $\mathcal{L} = \| s(\Tilde{x} , t;\theta) - \epsilon \|^2_2$, where the timestep $t$ indexes the noise level and can be mapped to corresponding noise level $\sigma_t$ through a predefined noise schedule and $\Tilde{x}$ is the image corrupted by $\epsilon$ during the forward diffusion process. This formulation avoids explicit noise-level reweighting and works well empirically. 

However, this simplification comes with limitations. While effective at aligning global statistics, it does not explicitly constrain the temporal behavior of the model or capture how perturbations evolve during generation. These issues are especially prominent in pruned models with reduced capacity. To address these challenges, we explore Jacobian-based losses that incorporate dynamical sensitivity, enabling improved temporal and geometric fidelity. We introduce these techniques in the next section.
\vspace{-2mm}
\section{Method}
\vspace{-2mm}
Given a pruned model $s(x, t; \theta)$, our objective is to effectively finetune it and minimize its performance gap relative to its dense counterpart $s_{\mathcal{D}}(x, t; \theta_{\mathcal{D}})$. To achieve this, we propose a hybrid loss function that integrates three complementary components: Noise Prediction ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{NP}}$), output-level Knowledge Distillation ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{KD}}$), and second-order Jacobian matching ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{Jac}}$). Each term addresses a specific challenge associated with finetuning the pruned models, particularly under reduced model capacity.
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{L}_{total} = \lambda_{\text{NP}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{NP}} +  \lambda_{\text{KD}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{KD}} + \lambda_{\text{Jac}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{Jac}}
\end{align}
\textbf{Noise prediction loss}. $\mathcal{L}_{\text{NP}} = E_{x, t} ||s(\Tilde{x}, t; \theta) - \epsilon||^2_2$. This is the standard training objective in DDPMs, which supervises the model to predict noise added during forward diffusion. However, for pruned models, directly predicting noise can be particularly challenging due to their limited capacity, especially early in finetuning when model outputs significantly deviate from the true scores.

\textbf{Output-level Knowledge Distillation (KD) loss}. $\mathcal{L}_{\text{KD}}  = E_{\Tilde{x}, t} ||s(\Tilde{x}, t; \theta) - s_{\mathcal{D}}(\Tilde{x}, t; \theta_{\mathcal{D}})||^2_2$. This term addresses the challenge of limited capacity in pruned models, particularly during the early stage of finetuning when learning from noisy targets directly can be unstable. By providing a smoother target derived from the dense teacher model, KD helps the pruned model learn meaningful intermediate representations and facilitates faster convergence.

While noise prediction and knowledge distillation provide essential supervision, the supervision provided by both objective is applied independently at each timestep and does not capture temporal dependencies across steps. As a result, it may insufficiently constrain the temporal evolution and local behavior of the score function, limiting the model’s ability to produce stable and coherent trajectories during sampling. This leaves a critical gap in capturing how perturbations propagate across timesteps—a key factor in the temporal stability of diffusion models. To address this, we introduce a Jacobian-based objective that explicitly regularizes the pruned model’s sensitivity to input perturbations throughout the denoising trajectory.

\textbf{Second-Order Jacobian Matching}. Diffusion models can be treated as discrete-time dynamical systems that progressively transform Gaussian noise into structured data through multiple denoising steps. At each step, the score model outputs $s(x, t; \theta)$ that defines the update direction and magnitude, while the Jacobian $J = \nabla_x s(x, t; \theta)$ captures the local sensitivity of the output with respect to the input. This sensitivity is critical in determining how small perturbations at each step affect the entire trajectory, especially in pruned models where reduced capacity can amplify inconsistencies.


To accurately replicate the dynamics of a dense teacher model, a pruned model must not only produce similar score but also exhibit similar sensitivity to perturbations throughout the sampling trajectory. This is especially important because even small difference in the Jacobian structure can lead to divergent sampling trajectories, compounding errors across diffusion steps. A seemingly reasonable approach to aligning the behavior of a pruned model with its dense counterpart is to directly match their Jacobian, i.e., minimize $||J - J_\mathcal{D}||^2_2$. However, this form of first-order Jacobian matching is fundamentally limited: it only constrains the model's instantaneous sensitivity at a single timestep, without considering how those perturbations evolve throughout the entire sampling trajectory. Moreover, such Jacobian matching becomes fundamentally equivalent to a combination of output-level KD with noisy input. Since DSM already corrupts input with noisy during training, output-level KD is equivalent to Jacobian matching
\begin{align}
    \sum_{i=1}^n (s(x+\zeta) - s_{\mathcal{D}}(x + \zeta))^2 
    = \sum_{i=1}^n (s(x) - s_{\mathcal{D}}(x))^2 + \sigma^2 \sum_{i=1}^n ||J - J_\mathcal{D}||^2_2 + \mathcal{O}(\sigma^4).
    \label{eq:taylor}
\end{align}
% This implies that explicit first-order Jacobian matching offers diminishing returns while incurring substantial cost.
Instead, we propose to align the second-order Jacobian ($J^{\top}J$) inspired by the FTLE, which quantifies how small perturbations grow or decay over finite time horizons. However, the whole Jacobian matrix is not accessible during training due to the high-dimensionality. Therefore, rather than comparing full $J^{\top} J$, we project the Jacobian onto normalized random directions $v \sim \mathcal{N} (0, I)$, yielding the directional expansion rate $||J\hat{v}||^2_2 = \hat{v}^{\top} J^{\top}  J \hat{v}$, where $\hat{v} = v / ||v||$ is the normalized $v$.
We define our second-order Jacobian matching loss as:
\begin{align}
    \mathcal{L}_{\text{Jac}} = E_{x, t, v} [(||J^{\top} \hat{v}||^2_2 - ||J^{\top}_{\mathcal{D}} \hat{v}||^2_2)]
\end{align}
This loss measures discrepancies in directional expansion or contraction behaviors between pruned and dense models. Practically, it is computed using vector-Jacobian products (VJPs), avoiding the need to calculate the full Jacobian matrix explicitly. VJPs can be efficiently implemented using autograd functionality available in most deep learning platforms, such as PyTorch. While random projections offer a tractable approximation, exploring more sophisticated strategies for probing sensitive directions could further enhance alignment, which we leave for future investigation.

By integrating second-order Jacobian matching, our unified loss ensures the pruned model aligns not only in score predictions but also in the geometric and temporal evolution of these scores. 

