We adopt Diff-Pruning~\citep{fang2023structural} as our primary pruning method. To ensure fair comparisons, we use the same architecture and training schedules as the perspective baseline works~\citep{fang2023structural, bao2023all}, using 100-step DDIM sampling for U-Net and 1000-step Euler-Maruyama SDE sampling for U-ViT. Additional implementation details and training configurations are provided in the supplementary material.
\vspace{-1mm}
\subsection{Comparison with SOTA methods on U-Net based models}
\vspace{-1mm}
We evaluate IGSM on several datasets using U-Net-based diffusion models and compare against SOTA compression and caching methods, including Diff-Pruning~\citep{fang2023structural}, Deep Cache~\citep{ma2024deepcache}, and O2MKD~\citep{zhang2024accelerating}. Table.~\ref{tab:unet} presents results on CIFAR-10 ($32\times32$) and LSUN-Church ($256\times 256$), and LSUN-Bedroom($256\times 256$), including FID, parameter count, MACs, inference speed, and training cost.

On CIFAR-10, IGSM achieves the best FID ($\textbf{4.58}$) among all methods, outperforming Deep Cache ($4.70$) and O2MKD ($4.62$), while using significantly fewer parameters. Compared to Diff-Pruning, IGSM uses the same number of parameters and MACs but achieves a substantial improvement in FID ($4.58$ vs. $5.29$), highlighting the effectiveness of our proposed finetuning objective.

IGSM also generalizes well to higher-resolution datasets. On LSUN-Church, IGSM achieves FID of $\textbf{11.25}$, outperforming both Diff-Pruning ($13.90$) and Deep Cache ($13.68$), and even slightly surpassing O2MKD ($11.74$) while using $75\%$ fewer parameters than O2MKD. On LSUN-Bedroom, IGSM reaches FID of $\textbf{9.68}$, outperforming the baseline Diff-Pruning ($17.90$) by a large margin. While Deep Cache achieves marginally better FID ($9.49$), it requires nearly $\times 2$ number of parameters and incurs additional memory overhead during inference due to feature caching.
\vspace{-1mm}
\subsection{Comparison with SOTA methods on Transformer-Based based models}
\vspace{-1mm}
We further evaluate IGSM on Transformer-based models using U-ViT as the backbone, and compare its performance against several state-of-the-art compression techniques, including Diff-Pruning, and SparseDM~\citep{wang2024sparsedm}. Table.~\ref{tab:transformer} shows results on CIFAR-10 ($32 \times 32$) and CelebA ($64 \times 64$).

On CIFAR-10, IGSM achieves FID of $\textbf{4.05}$, improving over Diff-Pruning ($4.63$) while using the same model size ($14.5M$ parameters) and MACs ($3.7G$). Compared to SparseDM ($3$:$4$), which achieves FID of $5.58$ with $4 \times$ speed, IGSM offers a better balance between generation quality and efficiency. SparseDM ($2$:$4$) reduces the FID to $3.81$ but doubles the parameter count to $22M$. On CelebA, IGSM attains FID of $\textbf{3.35}$, outperforming Diff-Pruning ($3.57$) and the two SparseDM variants ($5.05$ and $3.52$). Overall, IGSM delivers consistent improvements in sample quality while maintaining competitive efficiency. These results further support that IGSM is architecture-agnostic and can be applied broadly across both convolutional and attention-based diffusion models.
