\vspace{-1mm}
\subsection{Ablation Study}
\vspace{-1mm}
We conduct a comprehensive ablation study to assess 1.) the contribution of each component in IGSM, 2.) the sensitivity of performance to Jacobian matching loss weight $\lambda_{Jac}$, and 3.) the robustness of IGSM under different pruning ratios. These analyses collectively validate the importance of second-order Jacobian supervision and its integration into a unified finetuning framework.

\textbf{1. Component-wise Contribution.} To better understand the role of each component in IGSM, Table.~\ref{tab:contribution} presents a step-by-step breakdown. Starting with the standard noise prediction (NP) loss alone results in FID of $5.29$. Adding knowledge distillation (KD) improves the FID to $5.05$, showing the benefit of leveraging intermediate supervision from the dense teacher. Adding first-order Jacobian matching provides no further improvement (FID $5.14$), confirming that it overlaps with KD while incurring additional computational overhead. In contrast, replacing it with our second-order Jacobian matching yields a substantial improvement (FID $4.58$), demonstrating its effectiveness in aligning temporal sensitivity and guiding the dynamics of the pruned model.
