\textbf{2. Effect of Jacobian Loss Weight}. We investigate how the choice of Jacobian matching loss weight $\lambda_{Jac}$ impacts generative performance under different pruning ratios (PR). Table.~\ref{tab:jac_weight} shows the FID scores for pruning ratios of $0.44$, $0.56$, and $0.70$ across a range of $\lambda_{Jac}$ values. At moderate pruning (PR = $0.44$), the optimal FID ($4.58$) is achieved when $\lambda_{Jac} = 0.1$. Both lower values (e.g., $0.1$) and higher values (e.g., $1.0$) lead to performance degradation, indicating under- and over-regularization, respectively. This suggests the importance of choosing an appropriate regularization strength for second-order supervision. For stronger pruning scenarios (PR = $0.56$, $0.78$), smaller weights ($1e-2$ and $3e-5$ respectively) yield the best results, reflecting the need for more restrained regularization as pruning becomes more aggressive.
% Increasing the weight beyond this threshold (e.g., $\lambda_{Jac} = 1$) results in degraded performance due to over-regularization. At PR = $0.56$, a smaller weight ($\lambda_{Jac} = 1e-2$) yields the best result (FID = $5.54$), and at PR = $0.70$, the optimal value further decreases to $\lambda_{Jac} = 5e-3$, achieving a FID of $7.10$. These findings suggest that lower-capacity models require more conservative Jacobian regularization, while moderately pruned models can benefit from stronger second-order supervision.

