Notably, IGSM consistently outperforms DP across all levels of compression. For example, for the U-Net backbone, IGSM achieves FID of $5.54$ at a pruning ratio of $0.56$, improving upon DP's $6.36$. For tighter compression (e.g., $0.70$), IGSM obtains FID of $7.10$, whereas DP degrades to $9.33$. A similar trend is observed with U-ViT, where IGSM preserves performance closer to the dense baseline, particular at moderate to high pruning levels. As we can see from these experiments, the quality gap between IGSM and DP becomes more pronounced as the pruning ratio increases. This suggests that IGSM is especially effective when model capacity is significantly constrained, as its dynamics-aware supervision helps mitigate the instability and loss of fidelity introduced by aggressive pruning. These results highlight that IGSM is not only effective but also resilient to strong compression, making it a robust and architecture-agnostic finetuning strategy for pruned diffusion models.

\vspace{-2mm}
\section{Conclusion}
\vspace{-2mm}
In this work, we introduced IGSM, (\textbf{I}mproved \textbf{G}eometric and \textbf{S}ensitivity \textbf{M}atching), a unified, architecture-agnostic finetuning objective designed specifically to enhance pruned diffusion models. By integrating noise prediction, knowledge distillation, and second-order Jacobian matching inspired by FTLE, IGSM provides supervision not only on score prediction but also on the geometric behavior and temporal sensitivity of the model. Extensive evaluations demonstrate IGSM’s effectiveness across both U-Net and Transformer-based diffusion architectures, and on datasets spanning a range of resolutions, highlighting its potential as a versatile and robust tool for deploying high-quality, resource-efficient diffusion models. 

