\section{Singularity and Divergence:  a discovery bridge based on Information Theory.}
\label{sec6} 

\citet{donoho2024data} provides a thorough description of the famous Kurzweil’s singularity rhetoric \citep{kurzweil2005singularity}. The singularity is stated as “the time between notable events drops to zero”. We translate this principle into the language of our paper as {\em the time between notable DUD drops to zero}.

We  transform “notable” into “useful” to open the next discussion:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[] Are all data-universe discoveries {\em useful}? 
    \item[] What is the impact of this question on the debate related to singularity?
 \end{enumerate}
This concept is instead challenged by \citet{bengio2024machine} for the case of {\em mathematical discoveries} {on the basis of the impressive current state-of-the-art in AI.} 
\citet{bengio2024machine} introduce {mathematical discoveries} with a special focus on the ability to make compelling conjectures. This requires a training objective that finds an equilibrium between compression and generalization. The authors propose an information theory approach consisting of using compression principles from learning theory in order to make a postulate simpler to prove. For that, a useful body of theorems is defined as one that efficiently compresses the set of all “good” statements. Hence, a “good” theorem or conjecture helps to simplify and to describe other statements in very few steps. Therefore, compression reduces the complexity of mathematical reasoning, leading to greater generalization.

In the following of this section, we instantiate our theory. 
Let {\sc AMS} = Available Math Statements. Figure \ref{fig-AMS} illustrates the relation between complexity reduction and generalization through mathematical developments.

\begin{figure}[H]
\begin{tcolorbox}[colback=blue!2!white,colframe=yellow!70!black]
$$ \text{{\sc AMS}} \underbrace{\Longrightarrow}_{\text{{\sc COMPRESSION}}} \text{Complexity Reduction} \underbrace{\Longrightarrow}_{\text{{\sc TRIGGERS}}} \text{Generalization} $$
\end{tcolorbox}
\caption{{From AMS to generalization}} \label{fig-AMS}
\end{figure}
We define {\em divergence} between (sub) disciplines {as} the situation where the disciplines cannot communicate to each other (anymore). \\
We claim  that there is a potential threat of divergence between computational and foundational EDS.  We accept Donoho's singularity within computational EDS. Yet, we claim that we are far from this singularity within the foundational EDS. For instance, mathematical discoveries go at a lower pace than computational ones. Such a “time discrepancy” should be taken into serious consideration. To illustrate the concept, it will be useful to define computational and foundational DUDs as those coming from, respectively, computational and foundational EDS.

We claim that, to achieve similar time lags between discoveries (and mitigate divergence) in computational and foundational data sciences,  only {\em useful} discovery timings can be compared. 
How to define usefulness in data universe discoveries? \\ 
A potential path might be to borrow the \citet{bengio2024machine} criterion on the basis of information theory. 
We follow this idea to rate the set of useful math discoveries performed by DS agents (whether humans or not). Here, the steps are a bit more intricate. 

 Then, we claim that useful DUDs trigger CAMS, with “C” standing for conditional. See Figure \ref{fig-DUD} for illustration. 
