\subsection{Analysis}


\paragraph{Compute cost versus performance}

\begin{wrapfigure}[20]{t}{0.49\textwidth}  
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.46\textwidth]{perplexity_vs_gpums.pdf}
    \caption{Sparsity vs performance tradeoff across different levels of compression on Llama-3.1-70B. Sparsity (\%) indicates how much width is removed from weight matrices. While 10\% sparsity does not improve compute cost due to residual parameterization, after 20\%, real world compute cost reduction can be observed.}
    \label{fig:latency}
\end{wrapfigure}


As our method is intended to reduce memory use and improve latency, we measure the total compute used in inference: We finding the minimum number of 32GB V100 GPUs our compressed model can run on without GPU-offloading, and measure the latency in ms/token during inference on sequences of 128 tokens \cite{ashkboos2024slicegpt}. 
This gives us the total compute cost in GPUms/token. 
We compress Llama-3.1-70B at different levels of width reduction, and evaluate both perplexity and inference cost, and report this trade-off in in Figure \ref{fig:latency}. After 20\% sparsity, compute cost is reduced from the original model, dictating the region of practical use of width reduction methods. 



\paragraph{Sinkhorn regularization parameter}


As stated, we select $\lambda = 0.1$ to serve as the regularization parameter found in Equation \ref{eq:1}. 
To understand the effect of different values of $\lambda$ on our method, we test three of our models on 5 different $\lambda$ values, ranging from 0.01 to 10.
As seen in Figure  \ref{fig:ablate_lambda}, the amount of regularization used does not change downstream language modeling performance to a large extent, suggesting that there are several acceptable weighting strategies for combining neurons successfully. 
However, different values of $\lambda$ can result in some perplexity differences in the Llama-3.1-8B model as seen at $\lambda=0.01$. Given training differences, different values of $\lambda$ may be optimal for different models, but the values presented here reflect an acceptable range.

\paragraph{Calibration Data}

Much like previous work that examines the behavior of neurons to dictate neuron alignment or pruning \cite{singh2020model, ainsworthgit, ashkboos2024slicegpt}, our method uses a small amount of relevant calibration data (~262K tokens) to determine the behavior of individual neurons. 
We evaluate the sensitivity of our method to the amount of calibration data used by testing the use of only 65K tokens, 131K tokens, 262K tokens, and 524K tokens to compute transportation maps. Results are shown in Figure \ref{fig:ablate_data_amt}.


