\section{Constrained Rewriting}
\label{sec:constrained rewriting}

We start this section
by introducing most general rewriting.
After that we show commutativity between rewrite steps and equivalence
transformations, however, in the beginning only for 
pattern-general existentially constrained terms.
Then we introduce left-value-free rules~\cite{Kop17} in our setting and discuss their rewriting behavior.
This prepares us to show a general commutation theorem between rewrite steps using left-value-free rules 
and the equivalence transformation.
We end this section with a comparison between
our formalism of constrained rewriting 
and the current state-of-the-art.

\subsection{Most General Constrained Rewriting for Left-Linear Rules}
\label{ssec:most general rewriting left-linear rules}

%NEW FORMALISM

Similar to constrained equations~\cite{ANS24},
we attach the respective set of logical variables explicitly to constrained rewrite rules.
Previously to this, rewriting relied heavily on the notion of logical variables ($\LVar(\ldots)$) for this.

\begin{definition}[Constrained Rewrite Rule]
A \emph{constrained rewrite rule} is a quadruple of two terms $\ell$, $r$, a set of variables
$Z$ and a logical constraint $\pi$, 
written as $\CRu{Z}{\ell}{r}{\pi}$,
where 
$\ell$ and $r$ are of the same sort
and $Z$ satisfies
$(\Var(r) \setminus \Var(\ell)) \cup \Var(\pi)  \subseteq Z\subseteq \Var(\ell, r, \pi)$.
%
When no confusion arises, we abbreviate constrained rewrite rules
by \emph{constrained rules} or just \emph{rules};
sometimes we attach a label to it, as $\rho\colon \CRu{Z}{\ell}{r}{\pi}$,
in order to ease referencing it.
%
Let $\rho\colon \CRu{Z}{\ell}{r}{\pi}$ be a constrained rule.
Then, $\rho$ is \emph{left-linear} if $\ell$ is linear;
the set of \emph{extra-variables} of $\rho$
is given by $\ExVar(\rho) = \Var(r) \setminus \Var(\ell)$.
\end{definition}

%%% TA: 
%%% extraneous-variables
%%% exterior-variables
%%% independent-variable, 
%%% non-parameter variables
%%% outer-variables, outside-variables
%%% uploaded-variables, incorporated-variables, introduced-variables 
%%% external-variables  outsider-variables outer-variables
%%% extentional-variables appended-variables undefined-variables
%%% indetermined-variables flexible-variables supplementary-variables 
%%% unused-variables 

Do not confuse the notation $\ExVar(\rho)$ with $\xE\xV\m{ar}(\rho) = \Var(r) \setminus
(\Var(\ell) \cup \Var(\pi))$ from~\cite{SM23,SMM24}.
It follows naturally, that a constrained rule  $\ell \R r~\CO{\varphi}$ as 
defined previously can easily be transformed into $\CRu{X}{\ell}{r}{\varphi}$
by taking $X = \LVar(\ell \R r~\CO{\varphi})$ ($:= \Var(\pi) \cup (\Var(r) \setminus \Var(\ell))$).
We also do not impose the constraint 
$\ell|_\epsilon \in \xFTe$ as it is done in~\cite{KN13frocos}, 
in order to deal with calculation steps via rule steps as in~\cite{SMM24}.
It turns out that certain complications arise if we deal with non-left-linear rules,
\emph{hence in the following we consider only left-linear constrained rules and 
leave the extension to non-left-linear rules for future work}.
%\textcolor{red}{give hints what goes wrong?}
% The extension to deal with non-left-linear rules 
% will be treated in \Cref{sec:Rewriting with Non-Left-Linear Rules}.

Before presenting the definition of most general constrained rewrite steps,
we introduce the notion of $\rho$-redex for a rule $\rho$.

\begin{definition}[$\rho$-Redex]
Let $\CTerm{X}{s}{\vec{x}}{\varphi}$ be a satisfiable existentially constrained term. Suppose
that $\rho\colon \CRu{Z}{\ell}{r}{\pi}$ is a left-linear constrained rule
satisfying $\Var(\rho) \cap \Var(s,\varphi) = \varnothing$. We say
\emph{$\CTerm{X}{s}{\vec{x}}{\varphi}$ has a $\rho$-redex at position $p \in
\Pos(s)$ using substitution $\gamma$} if the following is satisfied:
\textup{\Bfnum{1}}
$\Dom(\gamma) = \Var(\ell)$,
\textup{\Bfnum{2}}
$s|_p = \ell\gamma$,
\textup{\Bfnum{3}}
$\gamma(x) \in \Val \cup X$ for all $x \in \Var(\ell) \cap Z$, and
\textup{\Bfnum{4}}
$\vDash_{\xM} (\ECO{\vec{x}}{\varphi}) \Rightarrow (\ECO{\vec{z}}{\pi\gamma})$,
where $\SET{\vec{z}} = \Var(\pi) \setminus \Var(\ell)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Most General Rewrite Steps for Constrained Terms]
    Let $\CTerm{X}{s}{\vec{x}}{\varphi}$ be a satisfiable existentially constrained term.
    Suppose that $\rho\colon \CRu{Z}{\ell}{r}{\pi}$ is a left-linear constrained rule
    satisfying $\Var(\rho) \cap \Var(s,\varphi) = \varnothing$.
    Suppose $\CTerm{X}{s}{\vec{x}}{\varphi}$ has a $\rho$-redex 
    at position $p \in \Pos(s)$ using substitution $\gamma$.
    Then we have a rewrite step
    $\CTerm{X}{s}{\vec{x}}{\varphi} \R_\rho \CTerm{Y}{t}{\vec{y}}{\psi}$
    (or $\CTerm{X}{s}{\vec{x}}{\varphi} \R^p_{\rho,\gamma} \CTerm{Y}{t}{\vec{y}}{\psi}$
    with explicit $p$ and $\gamma$)
    where
\textup{\Bfnum{1.}}
        $t = s[r\gamma]$,
\textup{\Bfnum{2.}}
        $\psi = \varphi \land \pi\gamma$,
\textup{\Bfnum{3.}}
        $\SET{\vec{y}} = \Var(\psi) \setminus \Var(t)$, and
\textup{\Bfnum{4.}}
        $Y = \ExVar(\rho) \cup (X \cap \Var(t))$.
We call this notion \emph{most general rewrite steps}; the reason behind this naming 
will become clear in the remainder of the paper.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}
\label{exa:rewriting constrained terms}
Consider a constrained rule
$\rho\colon \CRu{\SET{x',y'}}{\m{f}(x')}{\m{g}(y')}{x' \ge \m{0} \land y' > x'}$.
The constrained term 
$\CTerm{\SET{x}}{\m{f}(x)}{}{x>\m{0}}$
has a $\rho$-redex at the root position using the 
substitution $\gamma = \SET{ x' \mapsto x }$---thus
observe that $\gamma(x') = x \in \SET{x}$
and $\vDash_\xM x > \m{0} \Rightarrow (\ECO{y'}{x \ge \m{0} \land y' > x)}$.
Therefore, we obtain a rewrite step 
$\CTerm{\SET{x}}{\m{f}(x)}{}{x>\m{0}} \R^\varepsilon_{\rho,\gamma}
\CTerm{\SET{y'}}{\m{g}(y')}{x}{x>\m{0} \land x \ge \m{0} \land y' > x}$. 
Note that
$(x' \ge \m{0} \land y' > x')\gamma = (x \ge \m{0} \land y' > x)$,
$\Var(x \ge \m{0} \land y' > x) \setminus \Var(\m{g}(y')) = \SET{x}$, and
$\ExVar(\rho) \cup (\SET{x} \cap \Var(\m{g}(y'))) = \SET{y'}$.
\end{example}

\begin{remark}
In comparison to the previous notion of constrained rewrite steps~\cite{KN13frocos},
in our new notion an instance of the constraint of the used rewrite rule is
attached to the constraint of the constrained term. Previously, the constraint
remained unchanged.
%
This implies that in each rewrite step a fresh variant of a constrained rewrite rule is needed
in order to prevent variable clashes.
%
Therefore, the constraint part of a constrained term 
may grow along rewriting sequences.
However, we expect that this does not cause any troubles in actual
implementations as rewrite steps can be combined with simplification of constraints.
%
For example, in \Cref{exa:rewriting constrained terms} one may perform simplifications
after the rewrite step:
\[
\CTerm{\SET{x}}{\m{f}(x)}{}{x>\m{0}} \R_{\rho}
\CTerm{\SET{y'}}{\m{g}(y')}{x}{x>\m{0} \land x \ge \m{0} \land y' > x}
\sim
\CTerm{\SET{y'}}{\m{g}(y')}{}{y'>\m{1}}
\]
In the following, we focus on the theoretical aspects of rewrite steps, hence
we do not consider simplifications of constraints.
\end{remark}

In a constrained rewrite step 
$\CTerm{X}{s}{\vec{x}}{\varphi} \to \CTerm{Y}{t}{\vec{y}}{\psi}$,
variables of $X$ will sometimes either be moved to $Y$ or $\SET{\vec{y}}$, or
even be eliminated such that they do not appear in $\CTerm{Y}{t}{\vec{y}}{\psi}$ anymore.
The latter behavior may happen in rewrite rules with eliminating variables,
i.e., where $\Var(\ell) \setminus \Var(r) \neq \varnothing$.
The following example illustrates such situations.

\begin{example}
%Let $\rho\colon \CRu{Z}{\ell}{r}{\pi}
Let $\rho\colon \CRu{Z}{\ell}{r}{\pi} = \CRu{\SET{v',w',x',y',z'}}{\m{f}(v',w',x',y',z')}{v'}{y' \geq \m{0}}$
be a constrained rule. Consider a rewrite step
\begin{align*}
\CTerm{X}{s}{\vec{x}}{\varphi}  
&= \CTerm{\SET{v,w,x,y,z}}{\m{g}(\m{f}(v,w,x,y,z),w)}{}{x \geq \m{0}}\\
&\R^{1}_{\rho,\gamma} \CTerm{\SET{v,w}}{\m{g}(v,w)}{x,y}{x > 1 \land y \geq \m{0}}
= \CTerm{Y}{t}{\vec{y}}{\psi}
\end{align*}
where and $\gamma = \SET{ v' \mapsto v, \ldots, z' \to z }$.
We trace now where the variables in $X$ move during this rewrite step:
%\begin{itemize}
%\item 
We have $v \in Y$ because
$v' \in\Var(r)$,
$\gamma(v') = v$ and
$v \in \Var(t)$.
%
%\item 
