\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

In this paper, we have revisited the formalism of constrained rewriting in LCTRSs.
We have introduced the new notions of existentially constrained terms 
and most general rewriting on them for left-linear LCTRSs. 
We have further introduced the notion of pattern-general existentially
constrained terms and an equivalent translation to them from arbitrary
constrained terms. Those were used as stepping stones to obtain a full characterization of
equivalence over existentially constrained terms. 
Based on such characterizations of equivalence, we have shown the uniqueness of reducts
for our formalism of constrained rewriting, and commutativity between rewrite steps and equivalence
with the restriction to left-value-free rewrite rules. 
Moreover, we have proven that left-value-free 
rewrite rules can simulate non-left-value-free rules if we include an equivalence
transformation at the end. 
In the final part, it was discussed in which way our new formalism of most general rewriting 
extracts the so called ``most general'' part of the corresponding original formalism.

Because of the expected complications, our focus in this paper was on rewriting
by left-linear rules. 
To deal with non-left-linear rules, we need to extend
the matching mechanism of the rewrite steps, however, this
complicates the definition of rewrite steps drastically. 
%one needs to deal with more complicated definition of rewrite steps.}
The remaining extension to the general case remains as future work.

