\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
% Background
There are often many different information systems that capture data about processes executed in an organisation. With digital transformation, the number of systems and the volume of data is growing further \cite{Lee2021UnderstandingDiscovery}. The data come in many different forms, ranging from master data describing a company's assets to quality measurements of a product/service delivered to a customer. Additionally, there is a high variation in sources, for example, a human manually entering a text or numeric value in a system, or the fully automated capturing of high-frequency sensor readings. All these data together can be used to describe the execution of processes, which can be used to analyse performance and quality issues and identify areas for improvement.

% Motivation
There is often a big gap between the raw data captured by the information systems and the relevant insights, or information, useful to a company~\cite{Rowley2007TheHierarchy}. Cleaning the data and deriving the `hidden' information is commonly done in a data pre-processing or data engineering activity, which can be challenging and time-consuming, as it typically requires ad hoc and manual effort \cite{Bilal2016BigTrends,Dogan2021MachineManufacturing}. It also requires domain knowledge, especially when analysing event data \cite{Schuster2022UtilizingReview}.

% In this work we consider a general manufacturing environment that includes a shop floor with several resources (like machines and operators) that execute operations on various products, which are possibly grouped in a production lot. The various events that occur related to the entities in the environment are registered by multiple systems. Each system only captures a subset of the complex production process and new information can be derived when the data stored in the different systems are combined.
% Later in this article we will define those concepts and their relations more formally using an ontology.

% Contribution and approach
To aid in this task, we propose a collection of patterns to derive `hidden' information from data. Patterns are common solutions to frequent problems~\cite{Alexander1977AConstruction}, in this case the problem of extracting information from event data. Patterns are successfully adopted in several other domains. Well-known work is the work by the `Gang of Four' \cite{Gamma1994DesignSoftware} and Fowler \cite{Fowler2002PatternsArchitecture} about patterns related to software design. Closer to the business process community are patterns in workflows \cite{VanderAalst2003WorkflowPatterns} and event logs \cite{Suriadi2017EventLogs}. Patterns can be used as a source of learning how to easily extract information and as a step towards automating the extraction. Ultimately, our research aims to improve the quality of the extracted information and the speed with which it is extracted. This paper is a first step towards that goal. The patterns described in this work are inspired by practical use cases, so we adopt the definition by Fowler \cite{Fowler1997AnalysisModels}: ``A pattern is an idea that has been useful in one practical context and will probably be useful in others."

% Patterns are a well-known way of creating practically valuable insights from data sets related to business processes. Such a knowledge- or domain-driven approach has advantages compared to existing data-centred techniques, for instance to discover specific patterns \cite{Schuster2022UtilizingReview}. Furthermore, if it is known what information is of interest, there is no need to spend effort and energy training a complex model in a data-centred approach \cite{Cao2010Domain-drivenProspects}. Looking at the problem of deriving information at a higher conceptual level, we can abstract and generalise patterns from company-specific data structures. These patterns then become relevant for a broader community that studies business processes. The notion of a pattern was first used in architecture to describe frequent problems and their solutions \cite{Alexander1977AConstruction}. Later, patterns were successfully adopted in several other domains. Well-known work is the work by the `Gang of Four' \cite{Gamma1994DesignSoftware} and Fowler \cite{Fowler2002PatternsArchitecture} about patterns related to software design. Other applications for which patterns are studied include business analysis \cite{Fowler1997AnalysisModels} and integration \cite{Hohpe2003EnterpriseSolutions}, identity and access management \cite{Nahar2022IntegratedArchitecture}, workflows \cite{VanderAalst2003WorkflowPatterns} and event logs \cite{Suriadi2017EventLogs}.

% The patterns to derive hidden information can be defined based on the expertise of domain professionals familiar with specific data sets. These experts understand the data patterns that can be used to derive hidden and valuable information. In this work, we define patterns on Event Knowledge Graphs (EKGs) \cite{Esser2021Multi-DimensionalDatabases,Fahland2022ProcessGraphs}, a model for multi-dimensional event data in which an event can be linked to multiple entities. EKGs have already been applied to data from discrete manufacturing companies \cite{Swevels2023InferringGraphs}, thus we focus on data sets and use cases from the discrete manufacturing domain.

We derive the patterns from observations about data sets from four different industrial production environments. To describe and formalise the patterns, we define a reference model. The reference model combines the well-known standard ISA-95 \cite{OPCFoundation2019OPCReference} and the Event Knowledge Graph formalism \cite{Esser2021Multi-DimensionalDatabases,Fahland2022ProcessGraphs}. The benefit of using this reference model is that it provides both a uniform terminology to describe the patterns and serves as a basis for extracting information from databases that are structured according to those standards. Subsequently, we define each of the patterns in terms of the reference model and motivate them by providing use cases. We also show in which of the four different industrial production environments that we studied the patterns are observed.

In summary, the contributions of this work are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Collected generic patterns for enriching event data from multiple discrete manufacturing data sets;
    \item Defined a reference model that describes the terminology;
    \item Formalized the patterns and illustrated their applications;
    \item Provided template queries to apply the patterns and an example data set in a public repository\footnote{\url{https://github.com/gitmpje/production-trace-patterns/}}.
\end{itemize}

