This paper presents SchedGuard, a temporal protection framework to defend against posterior schedule based attack using Linux containers.
Existing approaches based on schedule randomization were shown to be not effective against the type of attack under analysis. 
This defense prevents untrusted task from running and only runs trusted tasks within a certain period after the execution of victim task. 
We provide response time analysis for the suggested approach and propose a new security oriented scheduling policy.
SchedGuard is implemented in Linux kernel using cgroups so it can take advantage of containers.
The effectiveness of the suggested approach is evaluated using both simulation and real RC car on a embedded platform. 
The results show that SchedGuard can defend against posterior schedule attacks.
