\minisection{8) Poisoning a vast percentage of class exemplars is more important than corruption severity.}
Figure~\ref{fig:corruption_vs_pp} is an extension of Figure~\ref{fig:severity_results_pp_results} presented in the main body of this work. It shows (in accordance to observations stated in \textbf{4)}) that two conditions are necessary for a successful STP attack. Firstly, the corruption of the image itself cannot be negligible. Very low severity does not disrupt the training (\textbf{4A}). Secondly, the vast percentage of $T_p$ must be poisoned (\textbf{4B}). From those two conditions, the latter has greater significance. When poisoning $>= 90\%$ of class exemplars in $T_p$ performance decrease even with smaller severities of corruption. On the other hand, when less exemplars are poisoned the accuracy drop is negligible even for the strongest severity considered.

