wherein the magnetic harmonic component $\mathbf{h}(\theta, z)$ is obtained as
\begin{multline}
\label{eq:magnetic harmonic component}
\mathbf{h}(\theta, z) = \mathbf{h}_a e^{-\beta_a z} \tau(z) + \mathbf{h}_b e^{\beta_b (z+d)} \tau(-z-d) \\
+ \left[ \mathbf{h}^{(w^+)}  e^{\beta_w z} + \mathbf{h}^{(w^-)}  e^{-\beta_w z} + \hat{\mathbf{h}}^{(w^+)}  e^{k_0 z} + \hat{\mathbf{h}}^{(w^-)}  e^{-k_0 z} \right] \times
\\
\tau(z+d) \tau(-z)
\end{multline}
in which $\tau(z)$ is the unit step function. The first and the second terms formulate the magnetic harmonic in the air and seabed respectively, and the third term corresponds to the movement of two waves in the fluid in opposite directions. All unknown coefficients in \eqref{eq:magnetic harmonic component} are computed by applying boundary conditions. After some mathematical manipulations, one can verify that the unknown parameters $\mathbf{h}_a$ , $\mathbf{h}_b$ , $\mathbf{h}^{(w^+)} $ , $\mathbf{h}^{(w^-)} $ , $\hat{\mathbf{h}}^{(w^+)} $ , $\hat{\mathbf{h}}^{(w^-)} $ are found as follows

\begin{subequations}
\label{eq:betas}
