%\label{sec:Vessel parameters estimation}
Once the proposed DRNN/PIRNN estimation framework is trained, the evaluation phase can become active. % as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:resnet}.
This network is designed to deduce vessel parameters $\hat{\Pv}$, with a primary focus on vessel length, $\Ls$, by processing the scanned vessel magnetic wake samples $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{H}}$. The operational sequence of converting the measured samples $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{H}}$ to the data pair $(\boldsymbol{X},\boldsymbol{Y})$ is depicted in the blue-shaded region of Fig. \ref{fig:resnet}.

%The completion of the training process, utilizing the training-dataset specified in the previous sections, yields a DRNN/PIRNN model that serves as an estimator. 
%The labeled test set serves as the foundation for training the PIRNN. The input layer of PIRNN receives the known parameters ($\mathbf{X}$) of the problem \eqref{eq:main_int_eq}. The PIRNN then processes this input and generates an output ($\hat{Y}_{\text{DRNN}}(\mathbf{X}, \tau)$) representing its estimation of the vessel parameters. By comparing this estimated parameter with the actual vessel parameters (embedded in the labeled data), the training algorithm (e.g., Algorithm 1) adjusts the PIRNN's internal parameters ($\tau)$) to minimize the error between the predicted and actual values based on the loss function \eqref{eq:loss_pirnn}. This iterative training process allows the PIRNN to learn the complex relationship between the magnetic wake and the corresponding vessel parameters. After the training phase, the PIRNN can be employed to estimate the parameters of unseen vessels based solely on their magnetic wake signatures as depicted with blue and black arrows in Fig.\ref{fig:resnet}.

