\section{Conclusion}
This research demonstrates the efficacy of fusing physics-based modeling with deep learning for vessel length estimation by leveraging the vessel magnetic wake signatures.  
%The proposed approach offers a computationally efficient and robust methodology for vessel length estimation, leveraging a single airborne magnetic sensor scan, which effectively addresses the limitations of purely data-driven approaches.
%Nonlinear integral equations were derived to model the relationship between the magnetic wake and vessel length in a realistic, finite-depth marine environment. These hydromagnetic formulations were incorporated into the loss function of a physics-informed residual neural network (PIRNN), yielding a notable improvement in estimation accuracy compared to a standard deep residual neural network (DRNN).
By deriving nonlinear integral equations that describe the relationship between the magnetic wake and vessel length within a realistic, finite-depth marine environment, and subsequently embedding these hydromagnetic formulations into the loss function of a physics-informed residual neural network (PIRNN), a significant improvement in estimation accuracy was realized compared to using a standard deep residual neural network (DRNN). 
For example, for the vessel length higher than 100m, we have shown that the maximum estimation error among various simulation scenarios is 20\% and 5\% for the proposed DRNN and PIRNN solutions respectively.    
%While the DRNN-based solution exhibits a non-negligible error of around 20\% in average for various simulation scenarios, the proposed PIRNN scheme is shown 
%to demonstrated robust performance across a range of vessel speeds ($1-10  m/s $) and sensor scan angles ($0^\circ-15^\circ$). 
%Key findings include consistent length estimation errors below 5\% for vessels exceeding 100 m in length. For shorter vessels, acceptable accuracy was maintained, with errors generally below $10\% $ for scan angles less than $15^\circ$. 
The influence of sea depth on vessel length estimation error was also explored. For depths exceeding 1000m, the mean estimation error remains consistently constant for a given vessel speed. Conversely, a reduction in sea depth results in an improvement in the estimation accuracy.  



% This research establishes the effectiveness of combining physics-based modeling with deep learning for accurate vessel parameter estimation from its magnetic wake signatures. By deriving nonlinear integral equations describing the complex relationship between the magnetic wake and vessel parameters within a realistic, noisy, finite-depth marine environment, and subsequently incorporating these hydromagnetic principles into a physics-informed residual neural network (PIRNN), a substantial improvement in accuracy was achieved compared to a standard deep residual neural network (DRNN). The DRNN, constrained by complex parameter relationships and limited training data, exhibited a persistent error  of 0.2. Conversely, the PIRNN demonstrated robust performance across vessel speeds from 1 to 10 m/s and sensor scan angles from $0^\circ$ to $15^\circ$. Key findings include consistent length estimation errors below 5\% for vessels longer than 100 m. For shorter vessels, acceptable accuracy was maintained, with errors generally below 10\% for scan angles less than $15^\circ$ . Even in challenging low-speed scenarios (below 5 m/s), the PIRNN achieved a maximum estimation error of  at a $0^\circ$ scan angle. Furthermore, the influence of sea depth on estimation error was evaluated, revealing that for depths exceeding 1000 m, the mean error rate remains constant for a given vessel speed, while shallower depths correlate with improved accuracy. This synergistic, single-scan approach, using an airborne magnetic sensor, provides a computationally efficient and robust methodology for comprehensive vessel profiling, overcoming the limitations of purely data-driven methods. 



