\subsection{Evaluation and Comparison with State-of-the-Art Models}
To evaluate our proposed method, we compare it against two state-of-the-art speech editing systems: FluentSpeech \cite{jiang2023fluentspeech} and VoiceCraft \cite{peng2024voicecraft}. FluentSpeech is trained on the same LibriTTS clean data as our model, using the official implementation\footnote{\scriptsize{\texttt{\url{https://github.com/Zain-Jiang/Speech-Editing-Toolkit}}}}. VoiceCraft utilizes the 830M parameter pretrained model\footnote{\scriptsize{\texttt{\url{https://github.com/jasonppy/VoiceCraft}}}} trained on the large-scale GigaSpeech XL dataset \cite{chen21o_interspeech}.

As shown in Table~\ref{table:performance}, our model achieves the best overall performance on LibriTTS across all objective and subjective metrics. On GigaSpeech, it outperforms FluentSpeech consistently and improves upon VoiceCraft in three out of four metrics, while exhibiting only slightly lower SIM. This is notable given that our model is trained without access to large-scale training data. To support subjective evaluation, corresponding audio samples are available online\footnote{\scriptsize{\texttt{\url{https://rlataewoo.github.io/ttt-editor}}}}. 

To analyze the effect of test-time training (TTT), we conduct ablation studies by removing TTT from the duration predictor (DP), the spectrogram denoiser (SD), or both modules. Disabling TTT for the duration predictor increases MCD, likely due to unnatural speech rhythm that in turn degrades spectral quality. Removing TTT for SD leads to reduced speaker similarity, indicating that TTT enhances acoustic coherence. When TTT is removed entirely, performance drops across all metrics, underscoring the importance of test-time adaptation for robust speech editing in the wild. 
% \vspace{-10pt}

\subsection{Ablation Studies on Test-Time Training Components}
We perform ablation studies on the GigaSpeech test set to assess the contribution of individual components in our instance-specific test-time training (TTT) framework, focusing on the duration predictor and the spectrogram denoiser.
\subsubsection{Duration Predictor} 
Table~\ref{table:ablation_ttt_dp} presents the results of TTT applied to the duration predictor. Removing only the mask-level duration loss results in minimal change in WER and SIM, but leads to noticeable degradation in MCD (5.64~$\rightarrow$~5.80) and CMOS (0~$\rightarrow$~$-$0.31). Further removing the sentence-level duration loss causes additional drops in WER (17.49~$\rightarrow$~17.91) and MCD (5.80~$\rightarrow$~6.08), indicating that sentence-level control helps stabilize temporal alignment at a global level. These findings highlight the complementary effects of both loss terms in enhancing prosodic consistency during TTT.

\subsubsection{Spectrogram Denoiser} 
As shown in Table~\ref{table:ablation_ttt_sd}, we further investigate the contributions of the spectrogram denoiser components in the TTT framework. Replacing the cross-entropy loss with CTC loss \cite{graves2006connectionist} leads to considerable degradation in WER (16.85~$\rightarrow$~18.76), SIM (0.711~$\rightarrow$~0.696), and CMOS (0~$\rightarrow$~$-$0.54). Furthermore, removing the phoneme classification branch entirely results in even worse performance, with WER increasing to 20.77 and CMOS dropping to $-$0.59. These results highlight the importance of phoneme classification for intelligibility and acoustic consistency in real-world conditions.
\vspace{-5pt}

\subsection{Visualizations}
We first examine the controllability of speech rate enabled by applying TTT to the duration predictor, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig2}. By varying the mask length to 186, 306, and 442 frames, we observe distinct variations in speech rate, achieved without the need for explicit duration control modules as used in prior work~\cite{kim2024masked, effendi2022duration}. These results demonstrate that TTT alone is sufficient for instance-specific prosodic control.

To further assess the effect of TTT, Fig.~\ref{fig3} illustrates its application to the spectrogram denoiser. In real-world scenarios, bandwidth mismatches between edited and unedited regions often result in perceptual discontinuities. Our method alleviates this issue by adapting the model to the acoustic conditions of the input, leading to smoother transitions and enhanced spectral coherence.

