We make three main contributions: (1) a computational framework for investigating the relationship between source frames (articles) and frames in associated responses (comments), (2) a frame classification model that effectively generalizes across different text types (news articles and reader comments) and topics, and (3) two data sets: a manually labeled evaluation corpus (FrAC) and a large-scale data set of articles and comments aligned by topic group and labeled with dominant frames, for reproducibility and supporting future research.
%and (4) we provide empirical analysis of media framing effects that can inform further interdisciplinary studies.



\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}
\paragraph{Cognitive Studies on Framing Effects} Cognitive research established that news frames significantly influence audience interpretation using controlled experimental designs, where participants were assigned to different framing conditions of the same issue and asked to report their thoughts. \citet{valkenburg1999} examined how news stories framed as conflict, human interest, attribution of responsibility, or economic consequences affected readers, finding that participants generally adopted news frames. However, \citet{price1997switching} showed that frames not only guide interpretation but also stimulate new thoughts and feelings not explicitly present in the original content. \citet{nelson1999issue} and \citet{shen2004effects} showed that framing primarily influences the importance of existing beliefs rather than changing the beliefs themselves, with frames most effective when resonating with pre-existing schemas. While producing valuable insights, these studies were restricted to small-scale experimental settings and narrow demographic representation. This limited their generalizability to framing effects in natural media environments with direct audience responses.

\paragraph{Media Framing Effects in Communication Studies}
\label{sec:comm_studies}
Communication researchers have attempted to bridge this gap by analyzing reader comments on online news articles, albeit with mixed results.
Several studies have found evidence of frame retention. \citet{kleut_framing_2021} analyzed online coverage of Serbian protests and showed that comments were influenced by the article's frames.  \citet{dargay2016} examined terrorism coverage in The New York Times, finding that commentators engaged with dominant frames of the articles, although they also introduced new frames.

Other studies have found no evidence of retention. \citet{holton_commenting_2014} analyzed online health coverage across major outlets and found no evidence of frame retention in comment threads, noting a disconnect between thematic news frames and predominantly episodic comment frames. \citet{muniz_shaping_2015} examined coverage of an oil company expropriation in Spanish online outlets and found that media frames did not produce corresponding effects in reader comments. Similarly, \citet{zhou_parsing_2007} found no framing effects in news about Chinese sociopolitical incidents. Finally, in climate change coverage, \citet{koteyko_climate_2013} found minimal retention of frames in reader comments on online articles from UK outlets, while \citet{hubner2023news} showed that retweets sharing climate news rarely replicated the original frames. 

There are several explanations for these inconsistencies, including small data sets, focus on a single topic, and the different adopted framing conceptualizations. In this study, we increase the data set size, keep the framing conceptualization fixed but apply it across eleven topics, in order to disentangle some of these confounds.
% The inconsistencies in the results of these studies might stem from the small-size, manually collected, topic-specific data, which is labor-intensive \citep{vangorp2007constructionist} and hinders generalizability and analysis across different topics.

\paragraph{Computational Approaches}
NLP approaches to framing have evolved from exploratory topic-modeling \citep{DiMaggio2013ExploitingAB} to structured classification tasks (for a review, see \citet{ali_survey_2022}). The Media Frame Corpus (MFC, \citet{card_media_2015}), based on \citet{boydstun_tracking_2014}, has been particularly influential, spurring various frame classification methods including probabilistic approaches (e.g. \citet{johnson_classification_2018}), neural networks (e.g. \citet{naderi2017classifying}), and pre-trained language models (e.g. \citet{khanehzar_framing_2021}). These computational approaches have analyzed frames at different granularities: headline level \citep{liu2019detecting, akyurek2020multi}, document level \citep{field_framing_2018, ji2017neuraldiscoursestructuretext, piskorski_multilingual_2023}, span level \citep{khanehzar_framing_2021}, sentence level \citep{naderi2017classifying, hartmann_issue_2019}, and paragraph level \citep{roy-goldwasser-2020-weakly}. %Recent work has extended to social media texts \citep{johnson_classification_2018, mendelsohn_modeling_2021} and multi-modal analysis incorporating visual cues \citep{arora_multi-modal_2025}.
Some studies have investigated the generalizability of the MFC to other domains and languages: \citet{khanehzar_modeling_2019} found reduced performance when applying fine-tuned BERT-based classifiers to different English contexts (Australian parliamentary speeches), while \citet{daffara2025generalizabilitymediaframescorpus} tested cross-linguistic transfer using both fine-tuned pre-trained language models and zero-shot LLM classification on Portuguese news articles. However, these studies either focused on cross-linguistic transfer, or on the {\it diagnosis} of the problem of cross-topic and genre generalization but fell short of offering a solution to the problem, which we do here.

% However, these generalizability studies have focused on cross-domain and cross-linguistic transfer within similar text types, without addressing the challenge of generalizing across different text genres, which is necessary for studying framing effects and is the focus of this work (news articles to reader comments).

%These computational approaches have predominantly focused on detecting the presence of frames in isolated textual units without examining the relationship between source framing and that of audience responses. 
% Moreover, while framing can manifest across different text types \citep{vallejo_connecting_2023}, the generalizability of frame classification models across fundamentally different textual genres - such as articles and related comments - remains unaddressed. 

\section{Research Questions}
\label{sec:rqs}
The gaps in existing research motivate three fundamental questions about media framing effects in readers comments:

\begin{compactenum}
    \item \textbf{To what extent are frames retained vs changed?} Previous studies have shown mixed results. We investigate at scale whether article frames consistently influence comment frames, and whether this influence varies systematically across different types of frames.
    \item \textbf{How do framing effects vary across topics?} Topic-specific studies cannot reveal whether framing effects represent universal phenomena or topic-specific patterns. We examine whether framing effects are consistent or vary based on the topic being discussed.
    \item \textbf{When readers do not retain the dominant frame of the article, how do they reframe it?} Understanding reframing patterns is crucial for comprehending how audiences actively reconstruct media messages. We examine systematic tendencies in how readers shift framing when they reject source frames.
\end{compactenum}

\section{Framework} \label{sec:framework} To address these research questions, we propose a scalable and automated framework for analyzing media framing effects (retention and reframing). 
% Our approach examines how framing manifests in source documents and which frames audiences use in their associated response documents across different topics, while being generalizable to new domains and text types. 


