First, to apply the MFC labels across our two text types (articles and comments), we need to define a granularity that is meaningful across both of them. While most of NLP studies on MFC looked at article-level dominant frames in the MFC, it is not feasible for comments (or other response documents), which are often brief and composed of single sentences. We therefore chose to use the sentence level -- the smallest shared textual unit between the two types of text.\footnote{\Cref{sec:dominant} explains how we derive a dominant document frame from sentence-level frames.}


Second, to improve robustness, we isolated highly confused frame labels in the original MFC data set. To do so, we first identified spans-level MFC annotations (of ${\geq}3$ words) to which two or more annotators assigned different frame labels. We examined such inconsistent annotations using confusion matrices -- both for all MFC data and separately for each topic included in the MFC corpus. We took a conservative approach by only merging frame labels that were confused consistently across all topics,\footnote{For example, ``Health and Safety'' and ``Legality, constitutionality and jurisprudence'' labels are frequently assigned to the same spans in ``Tobacco'' and ``Gun Control'', but not in other topics. In this case, we conclude that the confusion is due to the topic specifics rather than meaning of the label, and keep such semantically distinct labels separate.}  resulting in two merges of two frames (\Cref{tab:frames}).
% When consolidating confused labels, rather than using an arbitrary frequency threshold or proportional cutoffs (as done by \citet{hartmann_issue_2019}), we adhered to the following principles: first, the confusion should be frequent enough overall (i.e. we do not consolidate cases with minor confusions); second, the labels should be confused consistently across topics, rather than prone to co-occur sporadically in one of them. For example, ``Health'' and ``Legality'' labels are frequently assigned to the same spans in ``Tobacco'' topic, while not being confused in others. In such case, we conclude that the confusion is due to the topic specifics rather than meaning of the label, and keep such semantically distinct labels separate.
To further improve robustness of the label set, we removed the two least frequent classes (``External regulation and reputation'' and ``Capacity and resources'') which accounted for less than 0.7\% of the data. % and were used only in some topics.
We finally obtained 9 frame categories, plus a generic ``Other'' frame to account for instances that do not match any of our nine frames. %We therefore extracted 3,000 sentences from the MFC corpus where no annotator assigned any label.
