    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{topiceffects.pdf}
    \caption{Frame retention rates by topic across news outlets. Points above the diagonal line indicate higher retention in NYT, while points below indicate higher retention in SOCC. Topic names are shortened for enhanced visibility.}
    \label{fig:topiceffects}
\end{figure}
\subsection{Do Framing Effects Vary Across Topics?}
Here, we explore the effect of topics on framing effects. We cannot estimate topic effects from the observed data directly, because the effect of topics is confounded by the topic-specific frame distributions. To address this, we estimate the effect of article topic on frame retention using logistic regression mixed effects models. Given a document-response pair $i=(d,r)$:
\begin{align}
    y_i \sim \text{topic}_d + (1|\text{frame}_d) + (1|\text{id}_d), 
\end{align}
where $y_i$ is a binary indicator of frame retention ($\text{frame}_d{==}\text{frame}_r$). Document (article) topic ($\text{topic}_d$) is a fixed effect of topic on frame retention. And $(1|\text{frame}_d)$ and $(1|\text{id}_d)$ are fixed effects, controlling for the impact of the article frame ($\text{frame}_d$) and article id $(\text{id}_d)$, respectively.\footnote{We control for article ID due to repeated measurements, given that we have multiple comments per article.} We then obtain the marginal effects of $\text{topic}_d$ on $y$ from the fitted model, removing any influence of the random effects. The resulting probabilities of frame retention per topic are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:topiceffects} for NYT (y-axis) and SOCC (x-axis).


We find that framing effects vary by topic, but the degree of variance is much higher for NYT compared to SOCC suggesting that these patterns are more outlet specific than the framing-based variation discussed above and shown in Figure~\ref{fig:retention_type}. This offers an explanation for the inconsistent findings in previous single-topic studies. Some topics exhibit consistent retention patterns across outlets: Coverage of \textit{Trump \& Elections} demonstrates relatively high retention in both data sets (NYT: 31.3\%, SOCC: 42.7\%), while \textit{Healthcare} and \textit{Education} also show moderate to strong retention. International conflict topics are consistently more often reframed by the readers: \textit{Israel-Palestine} conflict and \textit{Syria \& IS} show the lowest retention rates across both sources, ranging between 18-24\%. Other topics reveal outlet-specific differences: \textit{Immigration} shows substantially higher frame retention in NYT than SOCC, as well as the coverage of the \textit{Russia-Ukraine} conflict.
% Complete results are provided in Appendix~\ref{ret_by_topic}.


\subsection{How Do Readers Reframe?}
\label{ssec:reframe}
Comments change away from the dominant article frame 50\% (NYT) or 63\% (SOCC) of the time, so a natural question is {\it how} readers reframe articles. Figure~\ref{fig:reframing} summarizes the flow of reframing in the NYT across all topics. Readers predominantly reframe issues into political terms, regardless of the original article framing, but a particularly large proportion of value-laden article frames (``Fairness and Equality'', ``Public Opinion'') are recast as ``Political and Policies''.\footnote{This does not necessarily mean, though, that readers ``politicize'' all articles -- ``Political and Policies'' frame is also the most reframed one, and its relative proportion in comments is actually lower than in the articles.} This pattern, albeit to a lesser degree, also emerges in SOCC (\Cref{fig:reframing_socc} in Appendix), where readers also often recast different frames into ``Economy''. 

We also investigated topic-specific patterns of reframing for topics that included at least 100 articles. For instance, in the case of \textit{Immigration} (Table~\ref{tab:immigration_reframing}), SOCC readers both reframe ``Cultural Identity'' into ``Political and Policies'' terms, and vice versa, suggesting a tight interconnection of these two perspectives. NYT readers, on the other hand, primarily reframe into `harder' perspectives like ``Economic'' and ``Legality and Crime'' and interestingly, among these top five reframings (as shown in the table), the ``Economic'' framing occurs only on the reader side. Table~\ref{tab:reframing_patterns} in the Appendix shows the analysis for three additional topics, revealing varying trends across topics and outlets.
