\section{Discussion}
% \textcolor{red}{\textit{Merge with Conclusion?}}
Unlike previous computational approaches to framing detection and analysis, our study is centered on a \textit{constructionist} understanding of framing as an interactive process where readers selectively engage, modify, and reconstruct media frames based on their own interpretative resources ~\citep{neuman1992common, scheufele1999framing, vangorp2007constructionist}. While existing computational methods treat framing as isolated classification tasks focused solely on source content, we examined the dynamic interaction between media framing and audience reception. In uncovering systematicities (RQ1; \Cref{fig:reframing}) and topic-specific differences (RQs 2-3) in a large-scale study, we bridged disconnect between large-scale yet isolated NLP approaches and small-scale communication studies that lack generalizability across topics and contexts.
% Our work represents the first computational approach in NLP to enable systematic analysis of framing effects across multiple topics, directly addressing longstanding inconsistencies in communication research. 
In applying one framing conceptualization across eleven topics, our findings suggest that inconsistent results in prior work stem from topic- and outlet-specific variation in framing effects.
% , with some topics consistently showing higher retention while others promote systematic reframing.
We also found consistent trends, such as value-laden frames (``Fairness and Equality'', ``Morality'') being more likely to be reframed. This is in line with theoretical formulations of framing, according to which individuals more readily accept frames that match their pre-existing values \citep{chong2007framing}.

Our analysis reveals outlet-specific trends — e.g., NYT readers' political reframing and SOCC readers' economic focus — likely reflect interactions between editorial stance, audience demographics, and national context. While frame-based patterns are relatively stable across outlets, topic effects vary more substantially. Since our comparison conflates country (US vs. Canada), editorial leaning (center-left vs. center-right), and time period (2012–2016 vs. 2017–2018), we refrain from strong interpretation. Future work should disentangle these factors to clarify framing dynamics and inform broader interdisciplinary research.

A key methodological contribution of our framework lies in demonstrating reliable frame classification across fundamentally different text genres -- from formal news articles to informal reader comments. This cross-genre generalization addresses a critical gap in computational framing analysis, where models typically operate within homogeneous text types. Our validation shows that sentence-level frame classification can successfully detect frames in structurally and stylistically different content, with strong performance on our human-annotated data set FrAC.

Our study builds on the MFC, a widely-used framework in NLP. Yet, it is by no means the only or the optimal framework, and it has been criticized for the broad nature and lack of theoretical grounding of its frames~\citep{ali_survey_2022}. We note that our method is framework-agnostic: alternative framing theories could be readily integrated, whether more generic frameworks (e.g., \citet{semetko2000framing}), or issue-specific taxonomies tailored to particular domains, a rich direction for future work. Similarly, our methods for dominant frame detection and sentence-level annotation, while supporting discovery of major trends, limit finer-grained framing analyses, which could be explored in future work.

Finally, our framework holds potential relevance for other NLP tasks including argumentation interpretation and generation, and even disinformation detection. The systematic patterns we identify in how audiences reconstruct frames could inform models of persuasive text generation, helping systems understand how different framings might be received and transformed by target audiences.


\section{Conclusion}
We introduced a scalable computational framework for analyzing media framing effects as an interactive process between source content and audience responses, grounded in a constructionist view on framing. Through analysis of over 3,000 news articles and 380,000 comments across two outlets and eleven topics, we demonstrate that readers reframe content at least as frequently as they retain original article frames. Frame retention varies systematically by frame type, with value-laden frames being more likely to be reframed, and when reframing occurs, readers consistently shift toward political interpretation regardless of the original framing. To support our analysis, we contributed a robust framing scheme from an established taxonomy, together with robust classifiers that work across different document types, providing a foundation for future research on media framing effects.

