\section{Simulation Results and Discussions}
\subsection{Experiment Settings}
We consider a UAV-assisted ISCC system with $M$=5 UAVs. We set the sensing transmit power $p_s$=30 dBm, communication transmit power $p_c$=20 dBm, communication bandwidth $B$=1.2 MHz, noise power spectral density $N_0$=-174 dBm/Hz, height $H$=10 m, chirp duration $T_d$=10 $\mu$s, chirp numbers $\lambda$=25, environmental parameters $\varphi$=12.08 and $\varsigma$=0.1139. Then, we use the simulator proposed in\cite{9593198} to generate five different human motion datasets: child/adult standing, child pacing, adult pacing, child walking, and adult walking. The spectrograms corresponding to different human motions can be seen in Fig.~\ref{dataset}. 
We employ ResNet18 as the classification model. The network commences with a large-kernel convolution and downsampling, followed by four groups of residual blocks that utilize pre-activation units to enhance information flow and training efficiency. As the network progresses, it incrementally increases the number of channels while decreasing spatial resolution. High-level features are extracted through a global average pooling layer, and the predicted class is ultimately output by a fully connected layer.

To demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed SFLSCC, we compared our scheme with the following schemes:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Ours: }This scheme is based on the proposed SFLSCC framework and the control algorithm presented in Section IV, jointly optimizing the UAV positions $\left\{\boldsymbol{u}_m\right\}$, client aggregation frequency $I$, the data sensing volume $b$, and the split point $L_c$.
    \item \textbf{Baseline 1: }This scheme considers that the client aggregation frequency $I$ is given, and jointly optimizes the UAV positions $\left\{\boldsymbol{u}_m\right\}$, the data sensing volume $b$, and the split point $L_c$, as discussed in Section IV.
    \item \textbf{Baseline 2: }This scheme considers that the split point $L_c$ is given, and jointly optimizes the client aggregation frequency $I$, the UAV positions $\left\{\boldsymbol{u}_m\right\}$, the data sensing volume $b$ as discussed in Section IV.
    \item \textbf{Baseline 3: }This scheme considers that the data sensing volume $b$ is set to its maximum, and jointly optimizes the UAV positions $\left\{\boldsymbol{u}_m\right\}$, the client aggregation frequency $I$, and the split point $L_c$, as discussed in Section IV.
\end{itemize}
\subsection{Experiment Analysis}
To demonstrate the performance improvement of our proposed optimization algorithm, we compare the convergence and the required energy consumption to achieve the specified model accuracy among different schemes, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig.3}. It shows that the proposed optimization algorithm significantly outperforms the baselines in terms of total energy consumption required for training, while achieving comparable convergence speed to baseline 3, which has the highest sensing quality.

As shown in Figs.~\ref{fig.4} and~\ref{fig.5}, we respectively analyze the impact of different decision variables on the energy consumption and convergence of SFLSCC. As shown in Figs.~\ref{4a} and ~\ref{5a}, the higher the client-side aggregation frequency (i.e., the smaller the value of $I$), the faster the model converges and the lower the total energy consumption required to reach the specified model convergence accuracy. Such improvements are attributed to the fact that the differences among the client-side sub-models are diminished through more frequent aggregations. As shown in Fig.~\ref{5b}, the shallower the split point (i.e., the smaller the value of $L_c$), the faster the model converges. This is because the majority of the model is held by the edge server, which aggregates the model updates in each round. Compared to deeper split points, this results in a more stable convergence process. Additionally, a shallower split point means that UAVs possess fewer model layers, thereby reducing the computational burden and the communication overhead required to send the client-side model to the server for aggregation. This phenomenon is also confirmed in Fig.~\ref{4b}. As shown in Figs.~\ref{4c} and~\ref{5c}, the larger the data sensing volume $b$, the faster the model converges, at the expense of increased energy consumption by the UAVs. 

The aforementioned experiments are all conducted under relatively ideal conditions. To investigate the impact of different environments on the performance of SFLSCC and thereby guide subsequent designs, we conduct experiments in suburban, urban, dense urban, and high-rise urban environments, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig.6}. Complex environments can deteriorate model convergence, as occlusions in such environments affect the probability of Los links, thereby impacting the quality of UAV sensing and communication channels.
