\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}
We introduce our notation using the standard Bayesian inverse problem, given in Equation \eqref{eqn: bayes}, as an example. Lowercase regular letters represent scalars, while lowercase boldface letters denote vector-valued quantities. Capital regular letters are used for matrices or tensors. We do not explicitly differentiate between random variables, their realizations, and deterministic variables, but we provide clarification when the context does not make it clear.
Let $\bx$ be a potentially high-dimensional input vector of a high-fidelity (HF), deterministic, computational, physics-based model $\Mhf$ (\eg given as a legacy code). We are particularly interested in, but the approach is not limited to, cases where $\bx$ represents a parameterization of a spatially varying field, such as they arise in finite element formulations.
We further denote with $\byhfi=\Mhf(\bx,\bci)$ the vector-valued outputs of the HF model at coordinate $\bci$/footnote{In particular, we mean a \emph{spatial coordinate} in the context of spatial reconstruction problems}, which are arranged row-wise to a matrix $\Yhf=\Mhf(\bx)$.
Similarly, we consider a lower-fidelity model (LF) with the input $\bx$ and denote with $\Ylf=\Mlf(\bx)$ the corresponding outputs at the same coordinates $C=\{\bci\}$.
Finally, we denote with $\Yobs$ the matrix of observations, each row $i$ of which is associated with coordinates $\bci$, and $\byhfi$.

In a Bayesian calibration problem, we seek the posterior distribution $\phf(\bx|\Yobs)$ of the model inputs given the observations $\Yobs$:
\begin{equation}
    \label{eqn: bayes}
    \underbrace{\phf(\bx|\Yobs)}_{\text{posterior}} = \frac{\int \overbrace{p(\Yobs|\Yhf)}^{\text{Noise model}} \cdot \overbrace{p(\Yhf|\bx)}^{\text{HF mapping}}\dd \byhf \cdot \overbrace{p(\bx)}^{\text{prior}}}{\int\int p(\Yobs|\Yhf)\cdot p(\Yhf|\bx)\dd\byhf \cdot p(\bx) \dd\bx}=\frac{\overbrace{\phf(\Yobs|\bx)}^{\text{likelihood}}\cdot \overbrace{p(\bx)}^{\text{prior}}}{\underbrace{\phf(\Yobs)}_{\text{evidence}}}
\end{equation}
We use subscripts such as HF (for high-fidelity), LF (for low-fidelity), or MF (for multi-fidelity) if the dependence of a distribution on a specific model is not directly apparent,   
as is the case with the likelihood $\phf(\Yobs|\bx)=\int p(\Yobs|\Yhf)~p(\Yhf|\bx)\dd\byhf$. In particular, we assume a deterministic simulation mapping $\Yhf=\Mhf(\bx)$, such that the HF conditional collapses to a Dirac distribution: $p(\Yhf|\bx)=\delta(\Yhf-\Mhf(\bx))$.
Furthermore, we define the logarithm of the likelihood as  $\lhf(\bx)=\log \phf(\Yobs|\bx)$, and similarly $\llf(\bx)=\log \plf(\Yobs|\bx)$
for the LF case, and $\lmf(\bx)=\log \pmf(\Yobs|\bx)$ 
for the BMFIA or multi-fidelity (MF) log-likelihood. 

% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{The Bayesian multi-fidelity inverse problem (BMFIA)}
\label{sec:bmfia_conti}

The key component in the proposed formulation is the introduction of the multi-fidelity likelihood, which allows us to avoid expensive HF likelihood evaluations. To this end, we introduce $\Zlf(\bx)$ which consists of the LF model outputs $\Ylf(\bx)$ and the evaluated input field $X(\bx)$, \ie $\Zlf(\bx):=[\Ylf(\bx), X(\bx)]$. Here, $X$ represents the values of the (spatial) input fields at the same spatial coordinates $\{\bc_i\}|_{i=1}^{n}$ at which the output fields $\Ylf(\bx)$ are evaluated, and $\bx$ is a high-dimensional parameterization of these (input) fields. The discrete input field values $X$ and the field's parameterization $\bx$ (our model input) are related by a finite element representation (see Remark \ref{rem: fe_approx_fields} or our associated publication \cite{nitzler2025highdimsvi} for more details).

\begin{remark}[Finite element representations of random fields]
\label{rem: fe_approx_fields}
Finite element (FE) representations offer a flexible, geometry-adaptable approach for approximating random fields. The localized FE basis leads to sparse matrices, simplifying adjoint computations, as gradients are only supported locally. Moreover, the posterior field's covariance, when approximated with the local FE bases, typically exhibits a diagonal-dominant structure, making Gaussian variational inference (see Section~\ref{sec: svi}) both efficient and scalable. In contrast to global expansions (e.g., Fourier or Karhunen–Lo\`eve), FE bases can represent arbitrary, high-frequency, or even discontinuous fields.

A finite element representation of a random (potentially vector-valued) field implies the following parameterization: 
\begin{subequations}
\begin{align}
    \label{eqn: rf_fe}
    \underbrace{\tilde{\bx}(\bx,\bc)}_{\substack{\text{Continuous}\\ \text{(scalar) field}}} &= \underbrace{S_\mathrm{ele}(\bc)}_{\substack{\text{Local}\\ \text{interpolation}\\ \text{matrix}}}\cdot\underbrace{\bxele}_{\substack{\text{Local}\\ \text{DoF}\\ \text{vector}}}\ ,\quad \mathrm{for}\ \bc\in\Omega_{\mathrm{ele}}\\ 
    \underbrace{\bxele}_{\substack{\text{Local}\\ \text{DoF}\\ \text{vector}}} &= \underbrace{\mathfrak{R}}_{\text{Restrictor}} [\underbrace{\bx}_{\substack{\text{Global}\\ \text{DoF}\\ \text{vector}}}] \\
    X(\bx)&:=\tilde{\bx}(\bx,C)=
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \tilde{\bx}(\bx,\bc_1)\\
    \vdots\\
    \tilde{\bx}(\bx,\bc_n)\\
    \end{bmatrix}\label{eqn: input_field} 
\end{align}
\end{subequations}
In Equation \eqref{eqn: rf_fe}, the local (element-wise) DoF vector $\bxele$ represents the discrete degrees of freedom for a specific finite element with local spatial support on the coordinate $\bc$. The local DoF vector $\bxele$ is extracted from the global DoF vector $\bx$ by a restriction operation (local to global DoF mapping), represented by the restriction operator $\mathfrak{R}$. Hence, $\bx$ holds, in the general case, the nodal values of the FE representation. In the Bayesian inverse problem, we infer the posterior distribution of the global DoF vector $\bx$. The input field matrix $X(\bx)$ (see Equation \eqref{eqn: input_field}) represents the row-wise collection of (interpolated) input field (vector) values $\tilde{\bx}(\bc)$ evaluated at the same spatial coordinate $\{\bc_i\}|_{i=1}^n$ as the spatial point in the output fields collected in $\Ylf$ or $\Yhf$.
\end{remark}
For each input field parameterization $\bx$ we predict the corresponding $\Yhf$ with the help of $\Zlf(\bx)$, \ie instead of $p(\Yhf|\bx)=\delta_{\Yhf}\left(\Yhf-\Mhf(\bx)\right)$ we employ $p(\Yhf|\Zlf(\bx))$. The latter accounts for the fact that $\Zlf$ does not necessarily contain all the information $\bx$ contains, in which case uncertainty with regards to $\Yhf$ is introduced \cite{bilionis2013solution}. This uncertainty gives rise to the multi-fidelity posterior $\pmf(\bx|\Yobs)$:
\be
\pmf(\bx|\Yobs) \propto \underbrace{\int \overbrace{p(\Yobs|\Yhf)}^{\substack{\text{conditional}\\ \text{noise model}}} \cdot\overbrace{p(\Yhf|\Zlf(\bx))}^{\substack{\text{multi-fidelity}\\ \text{conditional}}} \dd \byhf}_{\pmf\left(\Yobs|\Zlf(\bx)\right)}\cdot p(\bx)
\label{eqn:bmfia_conti}
\ee


We denote with $\lmf(\bx)=\log\left(\pmf\left(\Yobs|\Zlf(\bx)\right)\right)$ the logarithm of the  multi-fidelity likelihood  $\pmf\left(\Yobs|\Zlf(\bx)\right)=\int p(\Yobs|\Yhf) p(\Yhf|\Zlf(\bx)) \dd \byhf$
which replaces the expensive HF log-likelihood $\lhf(\bx)$. While its introduction introduces additional uncertainty, we note that each evaluation involves the much less expensive $\Zlf(\bx)$, making Bayesian inference much more manageable:

First, we filter the high-dimensional input vector $\bx$ through the LF model $\Ylf = \Mlf(\bx)$ (for the sake of compactness, we sometimes write just $\Ylf(\bx)$) to avoid resolving and approximating the (deterministic) complex and high-dimensional mapping $\bx\mapsto\Ylf$ (see Subfigure \ref{fig:bmfia_concept} a)) or $\bx\mapsto\Yhf$ (see Subfigure \ref{fig:bmfia_concept} b)) by a naive surrogate approach. The relationship between the LF output $\Ylf$ and the associated HF output $\Yhf$ (for the same input $\bx$) is statistically \emph{similar}, resulting in a simple low-dimensional functional embedding, which, however, becomes non-injective, leading to a loss of determinism in the representation as depicted in Subfigure \ref{fig:bmfia_concept} c). 
\emph{Crucially, the loss of determinism is not a drawback--it is, in fact, the central idea of our formulation.} The mapping $\Ylf \mapsto \Yhf$ becomes stochastic and non-injective, introducing an approximation or \emph{information loss} in the posterior compared to the deterministic mapping $\bx \mapsto \Yhf$. This loss manifests as a blurring of the resulting multi-fidelity posterior distribution. However, it does not introduce bias, and the induced approximation error is typically much smaller than the error incurred by directly learning the full high-dimensional mapping from $\bx$ to $\Yhf$—particularly in the \emph{small data regime} (for an analysis of errors, see Section \ref{sec:errors_and_extremes}).

Instead of attempting to learn a high-dimensional (deterministic) function, we explicitly approximate the \emph{multi-fidelity conditional distribution} $p(\Yhf|\bx) \approx p(\Yhf|\Zlf(\bx))$, which captures the stochastic relationship between the low-fidelity and high-fidelity model outputs (and input features). BMFIA employs a probabilistic regression approach to model this conditional distribution efficiently.

To better understand this approximation, we analyze it in two stages:
First, we consider the simplified case of conditioning only on the LF output $\Ylf$. This step \emph{drastically simplifies the learning task}, as learning the conditional distribution $p(\Yhf|\Ylf(\bx))$ is substantially more tractable than learning a surrogate directly from $\bx$ to $\Yhf$ (assuming direct sampling is prohibitive due to the high computational costs of $\Yhf=\Mhf(\bx)$, or short $\Yhf(\bx)$). The key reason is the statistical similarity between $\Yhf(\bx)$ and $\Ylf(\bx)$, which leads to lower conditional variance and thereby accelerates the learning process while mitigating the curse of dimensionality.
Second, to reduce the information loss introduced by filtering through $\Ylf$, we augment the multi-fidelity input by incorporating the interpolated representation $X$ of the input field. These additional features\footnote{\emph{Features} are informative quantities derived from the input, either as deterministic functions or as latent variables, that capture aspects of the data relevant for modeling the target distribution. In probabilistic models, features need not be uniquely defined or deterministic; instead, they may summarize uncertainty or structure that is useful for inferring $p(\Yhf|\Ylf)$.} are not used to reconstruct the deterministic mapping but rather to \emph{guide the probabilistic embedding}--providing complementary information such as low-frequency components of $X$ that help reduce the uncertainty in $p(\Yhf | \Ylf)$. The resulting enriched representation $\Zlf = [\Ylf, X]$ leads to a refined conditional distribution $p(\Yhf | \Zlf)$
with lower conditional variance and improved predictive accuracy. This effect is illustrated in Subfigures~\ref{fig:bmfia_concept}~d) and~e) for an artificial demonstration example of a two-dimensional function, given as a low-fidelity mapping ($f_{\mathrm{LF}}$) and a high-fidelity mapping ($f_{\mathrm{HF}}$) version. Here, the inclusion of input features leads to more informative predictive distributions while retaining the advantages of a low-dimensional, data-efficient probabilistic framework. The multi-fidelity representations of the original HF mapping are in the following denoted by $f_{\mathrm{MF},0}$ and $f_{\mathrm{MF},1}$ (more details about their meaning are given in Figure \ref{fig:bmfia_concept}).
\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.28]{bmfia_concept.png}
    \caption{Comparison of the mappings $f_{\mathrm{LF}}, f_{\mathrm{HF}}, f_{\mathrm{MF},0}, f_{\mathrm{MF},1}$ in a simplified setting where $\bx = [x_1, x_2]$ consists of only two parameters for visualization purposes. The figures show how the model output at a \emph{single spatial location} varies as a function of these two parameters. They should not be interpreted as spatial fields but rather as the variation of the function value at one fixed point in space. In contrast, the full high-dimensional setting involves spatially distributed inputs and outputs, such as $\Ylf$, $\Yhf$, and $X$, which are defined over all spatial coordinates: 
    Subfigure a) and b) show examples of complicated LF and HF parametric mappings from the parameters $x_1, x_2$ to the respective model output $f_{\mathrm{LF}}: \bx \mapsto \ylf$, and $f_{\mathrm{HF}}: \bx \mapsto \yhf$. Note that the LF is nonlinearly morphed, scaled, and shifted \wrt the HF output. The red dots (\usebox{\reddot}) help to track points in the parameter space (no spatial points) in the different mappings (\eg training points $\mathcal{D}$). Subfigure c) depicts the lower-dimensional multi-fidelity mapping $f_{\mathrm{MF},0}: \ylf(\bx)\mapsto\yhf(\bx)$, which filters the input $\bx$ through the LF model. This results in a much simpler functional embedding. Subfigures d) and e) expand on this idea by augmenting the multi-fidelity mapping by $x_1$, which results in an even simpler functional embedding $f_{\mathrm{MF},1}: [\ylf(\bx), x_1]\mapsto \yhf(\bx)$ with less conditional variance, here depicted in the form of its mean function and two times standard deviation function (color-coded). 
    }
    \label{fig:bmfia_concept}
\end{figure}

In Section \ref{sec:small_data_approx}, we discuss how good approximations of $p(\Yhf|\Zlf(\bx))$ can be obtained with a \emph{small amount} of HF-model evaluations.  While we discuss potential error sources and extreme cases more deeply in Section \ref{sec:errors_and_extremes}, we note at this stage that the utility of $p(\Yhf|\Zlf(\bx))$ does not depend on offsets between HF and LF models nor on the correlation (\ie linear dependence) between HF and LF model outputs as, \eg in Multi-Level Monte Carlo and other pertinent techniques \cite{tierney1999some,christen2005markov,scheichl2017quasi,giles2008multilevel, beskos2018multilevel,latz2018multilevel}.
