\subsubsection{Generation of training data}
\label{sec: training_data}
In the following, we discuss how the Gaussian multi-fidelity conditional of \refeqp{eq:like2} that links low and high-fidelity models is learned from small amounts of data.
We propose an iterative procedure according to which:
\bi
\item A relatively small (50 to 300 samples) initial data set is generated by drawing $\bx$-values from the prior $p(\bx)$.
\item The corresponding pairs of low $\Zlf(\bx)$ and high fidelity $\Yhf(\bx)$ outputs are used to train a model for the multi-fidelity conditional $p(\Yhf|\Zlf(\bx))$, which is subsequently used to obtain the multi-fidelity likelihood of \refeqp{eqn:margMFGauss} and posterior of \refeq{eqn:bmfia_conti}.
\item The latter is potentially used to draw additional samples  $\bx$ for which low and high-fidelity runs are carried out to enrich the training data and refine the associated densities.
\ei

An initial set of training data $\mathcal{D}=\{\Zlf_i,\Yhf_i\}$ for the multi-fidelity conditional is generated by first sampling a small number of input samples from the prior distribution $\bx_i\sim p(\bx)$. We then realize these samples via the corresponding LF and HF models $Y_{\mathrm{HF},i}=\Mhf(\bx_i),Y_{\mathrm{LF},i}=\Mlf(\bx_i)$. Usually, a small number of around 50 to 300 HF and LF forward simulations is sufficient (note the computationally demanding HF simulations) to learn an \emph{adequate} initial approximation of the multi-fidelity conditional. 
For implicit, hierarchical prior models $p(\bx)$, as used in this work (see Section \ref{sec: svi}), direct sampling from the prior is not possible. Instead, we propose a slightly modified strategy that samples from the joint formulation for the hierarchical prior (before the marginalization step). This strategy gives good results in practice and is best and easiest explained with the numerical examples in Section \ref{sec:demonstration}. 
In an iterative inference procedure, we refine the multi-fidelity conditional by sampling further $\bx_i$, \eg in batches of 10 HF and LF simulations, from the current posterior estimate after a specified number of iterations. See Section \ref{sec: algo_summary} and Algorithms \ref{alg:offline_bmfia} to \ref{alg:online_bmfia} for an overview of this iterative procedure. We standardize all data matrices for better training robustness and conduct the inverse standardization in the prediction phase.

