\subsubsection{Approximating the multi-fidelity conditional with a probabilistic convolutional autoencoder}
\label{sec:p_cae}
Accurate modeling of the conditional multi-fidelity distribution $p(\Yhf|\Zlf)$ represents one of the core challenges in Bayesian multi-fidelity inverse analysis (BMFIA). In contrast to conventional surrogate modeling strategies (as discussed in Section \ref{sec:introduction}), our objective is not a direct deterministic mapping from high-dimensional input parameters to high-fidelity (HF) outputs. Instead, we aim to learn a probabilistic conditional embedding between low-fidelity (LF) and HF outputs (and additional input features X), which we call the \emph{multi-fidelity conditional density} (see Section \ref{sec:bmfia_conti} on functional embeddings). To ensure stable inference with the proposed probabilistic regression model, we introduce the following requirements, which we discuss in detail below:
\begin{itemize}
\item Stable and smooth gradients of the probabilistic regression model \wrt $\bx$ 
\item Robustness and well-regularized in the \emph{small data regime} (simple but flexible enough)
\item A function-to-function mapping\footnote{By function-to-function mapping, we refer to the approximation of an operator $\mathcal{G}:\mathcal{X}\mapsto \mathcal{Y}$ that maps input functions to output functions. While more general operator learning approaches exist, we employ convolutional autoencoders acting on discretized function representations. The rationale behind this choice is discussed in detail below.} for efficient predictions (and training)
\item Preservation of spatial features
\item Robust and conservative estimation of conditional variance
\end{itemize}

To solve high-dimensional Bayesian inverse problems ($\dim(\bx)\gg 1000$), we exploit gradient-based inference approaches, specifically stochastic variational inference (SVI) \cite{hoffman2013stochastic,blei2017variational} (see also our associated publication \cite{nitzler2025highdimsvi} for an in-depth discussion on this topic for high-dimensional spatial reconstruction problems). In the case of BMFIA, this requires the gradient of the multi-fidelity log-likelihood $\lmf(\bx,\tau)$ in Equation \eqref{eqn:margMFGauss} \wrt the input variable $\bx$, which essentially translates to gradients of the multi-fidelity conditional \wrt $\bx$. These gradients should not be confused with the parameter gradients (for deep neural networks, these are the gradients of the training objective function \wrt the network weights) of the probabilistic machine learning model that approximates the multi-fidelity conditional. \textbf{Stable and accurate gradient computations through the multi-fidelity conditional} are indispensable but challenging in practice. Within the context of BMFIA, achieving smoothness and stability in the gradients of the conditional model \wrt $\bx$ is often more critical than maximizing point-wise predictive accuracy. In Sections~\ref{sec: poro_elastic} and Appendix~\ref{sec:counter_intuitive_mf}, we empirically demonstrate models that fulfill this requirement and contrast them with counterexamples.

We also demand a \textbf{function-to-function mapping to allow for efficient training and prediction}. Furthermore, at least for spatial Bayesian inverse analysis, the ability to \textbf{preserve and learn spatial features} is another requirement. \textbf{A robust estimation of the conditional distribution}, especially its variance or even higher moments, is challenging in the small data regime. Many existing generative models tend to underestimate the (aleatory) variance. Hence, we propose parameterizing a sparse Gaussian (conditional) distribution directly instead (see Section \ref{sec:small_data_approx}). A Gaussian is a natural choice as it maximizes the entropy and, therefore, the potential bias in the model for continuous distributions with two moments and infinite support. The latter is advantageous in differentiation, even though the data support is, in practice, compact.

Several established probabilistic machine learning models can approximate function-to-function mappings, notably Fourier Neural Operators (FNOs) \cite{li2020fourier}, convolutional neural operators \cite{raonic2023convolutional}, or more general operator-learning frameworks for PDE-driven problems include \cite{kovachki2023neural} (please see also the Introduction \ref{sec:introduction} for a more complete review). Additionally, methods using fast Fourier convolutions \cite{fft_convolution} and image-to-image learning techniques such as physics-informed convolutional architectures \cite{zhu2019physics, winovich2019convpde} have shown promise. While variational convolutional autoencoders \cite{Kingma_Welling_2022}, conditional variational autoencoders \cite{mirza2014conditional,zhu2019physics}, and graph-based variational convolutional autoencoders \cite{niepert2016learning,pfaff2020learning} offer flexible and advanced modeling capabilities, we empirically encountered complications regarding gradient instabilities \wrt $\bx$, computational complexity, or underestimating conditional variance, especially in small-data scenarios. Although a detailed evaluation of these alternative models lies beyond the scope of this paper, we demonstrate some counterexamples that highlight these challenges in Sections~\ref{sec: poro_elastic}.

We found that a probabilistic convolutional autoencoder with a Gaussian output layer, illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:cae}, offers a good practical compromise between accuracy and complexity while also handling the laid-out smoothness constraints. The proposed model prioritizes gradient robustness and allows for several simple and easy-to-control regularization measures, which will be listed subsequently.
The model performs probabilistic regression between the composed low-fidelity output $\Zlf = [\Ylf, X]$ and the HF model output $\Yhf$. Within $\Zlf$, $\Ylf$ is the LF model output, and $X$ is the interpolated input field (at spatial locations $\{\bc_i\}|_{i=1}^{n}$) with its parameterization $\bx$. The probabilistic autoencoder predicts the mean $M(\Zlf)$ and variance $V(\Zlf)$ of a Gaussian distribution that approximates the multi-fidelity conditional:
\begin{equation}
\label{eqn:autoencoder_prob_layer}
p(\Yhf | \Zlf) \approx \mathcal{N}\left[\Yhf | M(\Zlf), \diag\left(V(\Zlf)\right))\right]
\end{equation}
For simplicity, we assume a square spatial domain with a regular grid structure, allowing the use of standard convolutions. However, this is not a limitation: the approach can be extended to irregular domains using graph-based autoencoder variants.
