% ---------------- generation of training data for GNN and GNN in general -----------------------------
\subsubsection{Probabilistic convolutional autoencoder and generation of training data}
\label{sec: porous_autoencoder}
For the \textbf{initial training phase of BMFIA}, we want to select suitable parameter candidates $\bx_i$ and obtain their corresponding outputs $\Yhf_i=\Mhf(\bx_i, C)$ and $\Ylf_i=\Mlf(\bx_i, C)$ by simulation \footnote{These simulations are embarrassingly parallel and  can be automatically performed by our freely available open-source software framework QUEENS \cite{queens}.}. To promote diversity in the training data, we draw samples $\bx_i$ from a \emph{modified prior} $\tilde{p}(\bx) = \int p(\bx | \delta) \cdot \tilde{p}(\delta) \cdot \mathrm{d}\delta$, where the original Gamma hyper-prior is replaced by a uniform distribution $\tilde{p}(\delta) = \mathcal{U}(a_\delta, b_\delta)$ over the precision parameter. This encourages a more space-filling distribution of training inputs and improves the robustness of the learned regression model \cite{nitzler_generalized_bmfmc}.
The bounds $a_\delta = 1 \cdot 10^{-3}$ and $b_\delta = 1 \cdot 10^{-2}$ are chosen heuristically based on the empirical distribution of prior samples. Since sampling from the prior is computationally inexpensive, we assess the resulting variability directly and select a range that yields a sufficiently rich and representative training dataset.
We furthermore use $\ntrain=100$ and two refinements of each $\nrefine=10$, after $100$ and $300$ SVI epochs, respectively.

We subsequently employ the probabilistic convolutional autoencoder (see Section \ref{sec:p_cae}) within the multi-fidelity likelihood function (see Equation \eqref{eqn:margMFGauss}) to \textbf{conduct inference in the inference phase of BMFIA}. The demonstrations in this section use again the previously introduced porous media flow model with its HF and two low-fidelity model variants \lfb and \lfm (see Equation \eqref{eqn: pressure_bcs}).
Figure \ref{fig: gaussian_nn_mapping} illustrates the predictive capabilities of the trained convolutional autoencoder (the multi-fidelity conditional) for three \lfb velocity fields that were not part of $\mathcal{D}$.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.35]{conditional_slices.pdf}
    \caption{Demonstration of the conditional multi-fidelity mapping ${p(\Yhf|\Zlf(\bx))\approx\mathcal{N}\left(\Yhf|M(\Zlf), \Kmat(\Zlf)\right)}$ (here: $\mathbb{R}^3\to\mathbb{R}^4$) for which we employ a probabilistic convolutional autoencoder, trained on (only) $\ntrain=100$ LF and HF simulations comprised in $\mathcal{D}:=\{\Zlf_i,\Yhf_i\}$. \textbf{Left box:} Three different \lfb  velocity outputs. \textbf{Middle box:} Predicted mean $M_i$ and (for clarity the) three times standard deviation of the probabilistic convolutional autoencoder (multi-fidelity conditional), approximating the HF output at the respective coordinate $\bc$. \textbf{Right box:} Corresponding HF model outputs for the same inputs $\bx_i$ are normally not calculated and only shown for comparison.}
    \label{fig: gaussian_nn_mapping}
\end{figure}
Despite the significant deviation between the \lfb and  the HF predictions, the multi-fidelity conditional enables one to obtain very good approximations of the HF velocity fields. 
We predict the two-dimensional mean vector and the diagonal covariance matrix for the associated two-dimensional HF output, resulting in a $\mathbb{R}^3\to\mathbb{R}^4$ mapping of each spatial coordinate. As pointed out in Section \ref{sec:bmfia_conti}, the relatively \emph{low dimension}, combined with a \emph{simple embedding of the training data $\mathcal{D}$}, allows learning the conditional with high accuracy even in a \emph{small data regime}. Learning a direct surrogate for the map $\bx\mapsto\Yhf$ without exploiting the LF model would not be expedient due to the curse of dimensionality. For the next and  more complex numerical illustration in Section \ref{sec: poro_elastic}, we actually show results of such a strategy in Appendix \ref{sec:counter_intuitive_mf}.

% -------------------- SVI procedure for the example --------------------
\subsubsection{Employed SVI procedure}
We use sparse SVI with VB-EM steps according to Section \ref{sec: svi} to iteratively compute an approximation to the multi-fidelity posterior with both LF models, \lfb and \lfm (see Equation \eqref{eqn: pressure_bcs}). Table \ref{tab:bmfia_settings} summarizes the settings. We provide the SVI procedure's convergence plots and iteration data in Appendix \ref{sec:convergence_plots} and Figure \ref{fig: svi_convergence_ex1}.
