% ----- Inverse problems, ground truth, observables
\subsubsection{Bayesian inverse problem, BMFIA setup, ground-truth, and observables}
\label{sec: setup_poro_elastic}
We investigate the following Bayesian inverse problem: 
Given are noisy observations $\Yobs$ (quantitative details below) of the previously described poro-elastic problem's vector-valued velocity field over time, we want to infer the permeability field, \ie obtain the fields posterior $p(\bx|\Yobs)$. 
We employ BMFIA to find a multi-fidelity posterior approximation $\pmf(\bx|\Yobs)$.

In analogy to the previous demonstration, we generate a ground-truth permeability field and the associated artificial and noise-corrupted simulation outputs that serve as our observables $\Yobs$. This allows us to benchmark the BMFIA posterior for the permeability field, as, in this coupled example, the HF posterior is unavailable.
We realize the ground-truth permeability field $\txgt(\bxgt,\bc)$, with its parameterization $\bxgt$, (input for the inverse problem) depicted in Figure \ref{fig:poro_el_gt} by generating a random sample from a Gaussian Markov prior field with a constant mean function of $0.5$ and a precision parameter of $\delta=6.0\cdot10^{-4}$ (see Equations \eqref{eqn: marginalized_prior} to \eqref{eqn: marginalized_prior_last} for more details). The resulting permeability field is then processed by exponential transformation $k_{\mathrm{gt}}(\bxgt,\bc) =\exp\left(\txgt(\bxgt,\bc)\right)$ to be strictly positive.
We obtain the ground-truth outputs (particularly the velocity vector field, which serves as the basis of our observables) by simulating the governing equations \eqref{eqn: strong_form_poroelasticity_a} to \eqref{eqn: poro_elastic_bcs_e} with the finite element method using our freely available multi-physics code 4C \cite{4C}.
The artificial observables $\Yobs$ are generated as follows: We select the time $t=1.3$ and record the ground-truth velocity field on a uniform $50 \times 50$ pixel grid 
in the undeformed, reference configuration. We subsequently corrupt the velocity field by \iid additive, Gaussian noise with an SNR of 50 (see Equation \eqref{eqn: observation_darcy_1} to \eqref{eqn: observation_darcy_2}), which corresponds to a medium noise level. The ground-truth outputs in their deformed configurations at several time points and the observed, noise-corrupted velocity field at $t=1.3$ are depicted in Figure \ref{fig: poro_elastic_observables}.
