\subsubsection{Practical notes on approximating the multi-fidelity conditional for more complex LF-HF relationships}
\label{sec:complex_mf}
Approximating the multi-fidelity conditional $p(\Yhf|\Zlf)$ for more challenging LF-HF relationships that emerge from very simplified physics in the LF model comes with many challenges. We want to highlight some technicalities and counterintuitive behavior that we already laid out in Section \ref{sec:p_cae} with concrete examples. First, Figure \ref{fig: poro_elastic_LF_HF_comp} demonstrates the vast deviation in the LF and HF flow fields for four random testing samples and the actual ground-truth for the permeability field (not part of the training), along with the mean prediction and three times the standard deviation prediction of the Gaussian convolution autoencoder model. The settings are specified in Table \ref{tab:bmfia_settings} and \ref{tab:deviating_bmfia_settings}. Note the fine-grained and localized differences in the flow fields in the HF model due to the additional nonlinear elastic coupling, which are not present in the LF model. The flow field of the porous elastic medium (HF) is not divergence-free (see Equations \eqref{eqn: strong_form_poroelasticity_a} to \eqref{eqn: poro_elastic_bcs_f}). It exhibits distinct source and sink effects, often very different from the LF model, with many localized complex features. Nevertheless, the Gaussian convolutional autoencoder captures the essence of the probabilistic embedding of the multi-fidelity relationship despite only using 300 HF and LF training samples.
