\begin{abstract}

For a sequence of $d$-dimensional request points under the $\ell_p$ norm, we reduce the offline version of the classical $k$-server problem (and its variant) to an instance of the minimum-cost partial bipartite matching problem on a graph defined on $d$-dimensional point sets $A$ and $B$ under the $\ell_p$ norm. We provide an $\tilde{O}(\min\{nk, n^{2-\frac{1}{2d+1}}\log \Delta\}\cdot \Phi(n))$ time algorithm to solve these instances.  Here, $\Delta$ is the spread of the point set, and $\Phi(n)$ is the query/update time of a $d$-dimensional dynamic weighted nearest neighbor data structure. We improve upon prior algorithms that require at least $\Omega(nk\Phi(n))$ time. The design of algorithms that make sub-quadratic queries to a weighted nearest-neighbor data structure and compute a minimum-cost (partial) bipartite matching, even for bounded spread instances is a major open problem in computational geometry. We answer this question positively at least for the instances generated by the offline $k$-server problem. 

Our algorithm hierarchically partitions points of $A\cup B$ into rectangles and maintains a partial minimum-cost matching where any point $b \in B$ is matched to another point $a\in A$ or to the boundary of the rectangle it resides in. It iteratively merges pairs of rectangles by erasing the common boundary between them and then recomputes the minimum-cost partial matching. This is repeated until all boundaries are erased and we obtain the desired minimum-cost partial matching of $A$ and $B$. By exploiting the geometry of the hierarchical partition, we show that each point participates in only $\tilde{O}(n^{1-\frac{1}{2d+1}}\log \Delta)$ number of augmenting paths, leading to a total execution time of $\tilde{O}(n^{2-\frac{1}{2d+1}}\Phi(n)\log \Delta)$.

We also show that, for the $\ell_1$ norm and $d$ dimensions, any algorithm that solves instances of the offline $n$-server problem with an exponential spread in $T(n)$ time can be used to compute minimum-cost bipartite matching in a complete graph defined on two $(d-1)$-dimensional point sets under the $\ell_1$ norm in $T(n)$ time. This suggests that removing spread from the execution time of our algorithm may be difficult as it immediately results in a sub-quadratic algorithm for bipartite matching under the $\ell_1$ norm.  






